1
00:00:05,207 --> 00:00:08,610
♪ In the not-too-distant future

2
00:00:08,643 --> 00:00:11,713
♪ Next Sunday A.D.

3
00:00:11,747 --> 00:00:14,815
♪ There was a guy named Joel

4
00:00:14,848 --> 00:00:17,552
♪ Not too different
from you or me

5
00:00:17,585 --> 00:00:20,588
♪ He worked at
Gizmonic Institute

6
00:00:20,622 --> 00:00:23,925
♪ Just another face
in a red jumpsuit

7
00:00:23,958 --> 00:00:26,593
♪ He did a good job
cleaning up the place

8
00:00:26,627 --> 00:00:31,698
♪ But his bosses didn't like
him so they shot him into space

9
00:00:33,468 --> 00:00:36,370
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies

10
00:00:36,403 --> 00:00:39,440
♪ The worst we can
find (la-la-la)

11
00:00:39,473 --> 00:00:42,343
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all

12
00:00:42,377 --> 00:00:45,880
♪ And we'll monitor
his mind (la-la-la)

13
00:00:45,913 --> 00:00:48,550
♪ Now keep in mind
Joel can't control

14
00:00:48,583 --> 00:00:51,618
♪ Where the movies
begin or end (la-la-la)

15
00:00:51,652 --> 00:00:55,690
♪ Because he used
those special parts

16
00:00:55,723 --> 00:00:59,827
♪ To make his robot friends

17
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:01,830
♪ Robot roll call

18
00:01:01,863 --> 00:01:02,963
♪ Cambot
Pan left.

19
00:01:02,997 --> 00:01:04,198
♪ Gypsy
Hi, girl.

20
00:01:04,232 --> 00:01:06,100
♪ Tom Servo
What a cool guy.

21
00:01:06,133 --> 00:01:07,335
♪ Crow
A wisecracker.

22
00:01:07,368 --> 00:01:10,838
♪ If you're wondering
how he eats and breathes

23
00:01:10,872 --> 00:01:13,942
♪ And other science
facts (la-la-la)

24
00:01:13,975 --> 00:01:16,912
♪ Then repeat to
yourself it's just a show

25
00:01:16,945 --> 00:01:19,913
♪ I should really just relax

26
00:01:19,947 --> 00:01:24,785
♪ For Mystery Science
Theater 3000

27
00:01:26,855 --> 00:01:31,358
(doors closing hydraulically)

28
00:01:34,728 --> 00:01:36,965
Hey, hi everybody. Welcome
to the Satellite of Love.

29
00:01:36,998 --> 00:01:38,767
I'm Joel Robinson and
these are my Bloods,

30
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:40,202
Tom Servo and Crow.

31
00:01:40,235 --> 00:01:41,402
- Yo.
- Word.

32
00:01:41,435 --> 00:01:42,670
And we decided to
take the prefixes,

33
00:01:42,703 --> 00:01:45,507
root words and suffixes
of Mex-American food,

34
00:01:45,540 --> 00:01:47,708
add some new food
types, mix 'em all up

35
00:01:47,742 --> 00:01:49,977
and do what Madison Avenue
does and find a whole

36
00:01:50,010 --> 00:01:52,013
new way to love
the same old crap.

37
00:01:52,047 --> 00:01:53,380
Yeah, like the pundits say,

38
00:01:53,413 --> 00:01:54,648
invent a new kind
of Mexican junk food

39
00:01:54,682 --> 00:01:56,918
and the world will make
a run for your border.

40
00:01:56,952 --> 00:01:58,987
Right, check
this one out, okay?

41
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:02,490
Oh look, spicy,
peachy, battered changa.

42
00:02:02,523 --> 00:02:05,427
Oh, that would a spicy,
lightly-battered peach

43
00:02:05,460 --> 00:02:07,195
in a delicious, chewy-changa.

44
00:02:07,228 --> 00:02:08,697
What the hell is a changa?

45
00:02:08,730 --> 00:02:10,732
Hey, come on.
Leave him alone, he's
just getting into it.

46
00:02:10,765 --> 00:02:11,867
Try this one, Crow.

47
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:14,602
Oh, chicken-y Gatorade-arita.

48
00:02:14,635 --> 00:02:16,637
Crispy dead chicken,
Gatorade marinade,

49
00:02:16,670 --> 00:02:18,907
all wrapped in a tasty,
toasty tacorito pita.

50
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:21,742
Oh! Okay, what
do we have here?

51
00:02:21,775 --> 00:02:23,912
Oh look, bel grande, cheesy--

52
00:02:23,945 --> 00:02:25,247
Did we use cheesy yet?

53
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:26,848
Yeah, well not
yet, here it is.

54
00:02:26,882 --> 00:02:30,218
Okay, beefy blue steak taco.

55
00:02:30,252 --> 00:02:31,152
Oh good, we're on a roll.

56
00:02:31,185 --> 00:02:35,257
Bel rocco, poco, loco, roccoco--

57
00:02:35,290 --> 00:02:36,657
Hey, I'm running out of room.

58
00:02:36,690 --> 00:02:38,727
That's okay, keep flowin'
with it. Taco! (laughs)

59
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:40,462
Ooh, it's cocomoco good!

60
00:02:40,495 --> 00:02:41,462
[all] Olé!

61
00:02:41,495 --> 00:02:43,765
Olé off. Commercial
sign in 15 seconds.

62
00:02:43,798 --> 00:02:46,968
Oh, well here's my favorite
right here. Check this out.

63
00:02:47,002 --> 00:02:50,372
Beefy, peanut
buster, bel grande!

64
00:02:50,405 --> 00:02:53,173
Oh, you get a mister
misdemeanor for that beater.

65
00:02:53,207 --> 00:02:55,075
- Ore-Ida.
- Orita!

66
00:02:55,108 --> 00:02:56,677
Goodnight-a.

67
00:02:56,710 --> 00:03:00,882
(new wave music)

68
00:03:01,917 --> 00:03:05,353
Well, this one's
the Pat Morita fajita.

69
00:03:05,387 --> 00:03:07,155
(robots laugh)
That's cute.

70
00:03:07,188 --> 00:03:12,127
And then there's the
Maria Conchita Alonzarito.

71
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:13,460
Cute, cute,
that's a good one.

72
00:03:13,493 --> 00:03:14,462
Here's one of my favorites,

73
00:03:14,495 --> 00:03:19,733
the Emperor Hirohito-rito.

74
00:03:19,767 --> 00:03:23,838
And the Curly Joe Dorito.

75
00:03:23,872 --> 00:03:25,907
And not to forget the

76
00:03:25,940 --> 00:03:30,778
Ed Herlihy-ito Dorito.

77
00:03:30,812 --> 00:03:34,148
Made with fine
Kraft Jet-Puffed
miniature marshmallows.

78
00:03:34,182 --> 00:03:36,783
(laughs) Say Joel,
why do Americans

79
00:03:36,817 --> 00:03:38,552
rename Mexican food dishes?

80
00:03:38,587 --> 00:03:40,288
Oh, it's just part
of the American way.

81
00:03:40,322 --> 00:03:42,590
Turning a neighboring
country rich in

82
00:03:42,623 --> 00:03:45,260
culture and beauty
into a goofy appetizer.

83
00:03:45,293 --> 00:03:47,362
Oops, I think the
Mads are calling.

84
00:03:47,395 --> 00:03:50,532
Happy hour is
over, taco beelzebub.

85
00:03:50,565 --> 00:03:51,967
You know, in your
little food talk,

86
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:53,400
you failed to mention
that breakfast is still

87
00:03:53,433 --> 00:03:55,235
the most important
meal of the day.

88
00:03:55,270 --> 00:03:56,403
Isn't that right, Frank?

89
00:03:56,437 --> 00:03:57,805
Yes sir. As a matter
of fact, I usually try

90
00:03:57,838 --> 00:04:02,043
to have my breakfast first
thing in the morning (laughs).

91
00:04:02,077 --> 00:04:03,577
Which, for me is usually about

92
00:04:03,612 --> 00:04:07,948
2:30 in the afternoon (laughs).

93
00:04:07,982 --> 00:04:10,818
(laughs turn into cries)

94
00:04:10,852 --> 00:04:12,820
Oh God, it hurts.

95
00:04:12,853 --> 00:04:16,757
There, there. Why don't
you run along and play, Frank.

96
00:04:16,790 --> 00:04:18,927
Thanks, Steve.

97
00:04:18,960 --> 00:04:19,993
He's in therapy.

98
00:04:20,027 --> 00:04:23,732
Well anyway, our invention
exchange this week is

99
00:04:23,765 --> 00:04:26,700
for family members who
won't come in for breakfast.

100
00:04:26,733 --> 00:04:31,973
You bring breakfast to them
with the breakfast bazooka.

101
00:04:32,007 --> 00:04:34,875
Oh Frank, come in for breakfast!

102
00:04:34,908 --> 00:04:36,010
I'm playing!

103
00:04:36,043 --> 00:04:40,782
You die Joe!
(bazooka fires)

104
00:04:41,983 --> 00:04:43,785
This delicious meal
has a flat trajectory

105
00:04:43,818 --> 00:04:47,455
and a muzzle velocity of
2,400 feet per second.

106
00:04:47,488 --> 00:04:50,325
It's all part of
this nutritious war.

107
00:04:50,358 --> 00:04:51,458
What do you think?

108
00:04:51,492 --> 00:04:52,727
What a coinkydink.

109
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:54,695
Our invention is based
on a similar concept.

110
00:04:54,728 --> 00:04:56,430
That's right, it's for
the hurried youth of today,

111
00:04:56,463 --> 00:04:59,533
so busy, they sometimes
forget to stuff their faces

112
00:04:59,567 --> 00:05:02,803
with toxic, chemically-laced,
life-shortening sugar snacks.

113
00:05:02,837 --> 00:05:06,040
Yeah, it's called
a between-meal
mortar, check it out.

114
00:05:06,073 --> 00:05:08,810
Clear! (mortar fires)

115
00:05:08,843 --> 00:05:09,843
(all scream)

116
00:05:09,877 --> 00:05:10,678
It's a live one!

117
00:05:10,712 --> 00:05:13,113
Look out guys, I'll save you!

118
00:05:13,147 --> 00:05:14,548
(loud explosion)

119
00:05:14,582 --> 00:05:17,718
(all coughing and groaning)

120
00:05:17,752 --> 00:05:20,087
They say you never hear the
snack with your name on it.

121
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:21,555
What do you think, sirs?

122
00:05:21,588 --> 00:05:24,625
Well, Julia Childish,
your experiment this week

123
00:05:24,658 --> 00:05:28,495
is an unwanted, uncalled-for,
unnecessary sequel

124
00:05:28,528 --> 00:05:32,533
to The Amazing Colossal Man,
War of the Colossal Beast.

125
00:05:32,567 --> 00:05:34,935
But first, a little
musical nightmare

126
00:05:34,968 --> 00:05:36,837
from the heart of whiteness.

127
00:05:36,870 --> 00:05:38,238
Bon appe-die.

128
00:05:38,272 --> 00:05:39,673
Oh cripes,
these buffets on my

129
00:05:39,707 --> 00:05:41,742
body are so
reasonable, you know?

130
00:05:41,775 --> 00:05:42,743
You know, Marge
and I came here

131
00:05:42,777 --> 00:05:44,512
one time we brought the men in.

132
00:05:44,545 --> 00:05:45,747
Oh cripes, you gotta try some

133
00:05:45,780 --> 00:05:48,115
of their Rice Krispies
down by the belly button.

134
00:05:48,148 --> 00:05:49,283
(buzzing and screaming)

135
00:05:49,317 --> 00:05:51,718
Oh, we've got
movie sign! (screams)

136
00:05:51,752 --> 00:05:56,523
(doors opening hydraulically)

137
00:06:00,528 --> 00:06:01,662
(all coughing and spitting)

138
00:06:01,695 --> 00:06:02,863
Just get it out of
my valves, will you?

139
00:06:02,897 --> 00:06:06,000
Oh, Mr. Natural. I remember
him from Zap Comix.

140
00:06:06,033 --> 00:06:08,435
(marching band music)

141
00:06:08,468 --> 00:06:11,705
CONN LTD. and the
New Power Generation.

142
00:06:11,738 --> 00:06:14,308
Conn!

143
00:06:14,342 --> 00:06:17,578
And the Republican
High School marching band!

144
00:06:17,612 --> 00:06:19,880
Betty Luster, was she
married to Martin Sheen?

145
00:06:19,913 --> 00:06:22,183
Think so (laughs).

146
00:06:22,217 --> 00:06:23,717
Oh, look at that guy.

147
00:06:23,752 --> 00:06:26,153
Otto Krauschaar!

148
00:06:26,187 --> 00:06:28,555
What does A.S.C. mean?

149
00:06:28,588 --> 00:06:31,692
- A sick cookie.
- Oh.

150
00:06:31,725 --> 00:06:36,163
[all sing] Come on and
buy some crap from us.

151
00:06:36,197 --> 00:06:39,633
You know that you want to.

152
00:06:39,667 --> 00:06:43,137
And the white race
will salute you

153
00:06:43,170 --> 00:06:47,642
as you prance and gad about.

154
00:06:47,675 --> 00:06:48,777
Thank you.
(flute music)

155
00:06:48,810 --> 00:06:50,343
Huh?

156
00:06:50,377 --> 00:06:52,580
Boy, am I glad to see you!

157
00:06:52,613 --> 00:06:53,815
Well, it's not mutual.

158
00:06:53,848 --> 00:06:55,917
What's that? What do
you mean, "Who's talking"?

159
00:06:55,950 --> 00:06:56,783
Yeah.

160
00:06:56,817 --> 00:06:59,253
It's me, that's
who. Mr. B Natural.

161
00:06:59,287 --> 00:07:00,755
Second note from the left.

162
00:07:00,788 --> 00:07:02,523
As natural a B
as you'll ever see.

163
00:07:02,557 --> 00:07:03,523
Joel, I'm scared.

164
00:07:03,557 --> 00:07:05,827
Wait, hold the music!

165
00:07:05,860 --> 00:07:07,128
Oh, thank you.

166
00:07:07,162 --> 00:07:08,328
Maybe we can make things
a little clearer around here.

167
00:07:08,362 --> 00:07:10,363
(flute plays)
(all scream)

168
00:07:10,397 --> 00:07:11,798
- How's that?
- It stinks.

169
00:07:11,832 --> 00:07:13,700
Now, move in a
little closer, friend.

170
00:07:13,733 --> 00:07:15,903
- No, no, don't.
- Let's get acquainted.

171
00:07:15,937 --> 00:07:16,803
No, let's not.

172
00:07:16,837 --> 00:07:20,107
Mr. B Natural,
at your service.

173
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:22,210
The spirit of
music, that's me.

174
00:07:22,243 --> 00:07:23,777
The spirit of fun in music.

175
00:07:23,812 --> 00:07:24,778
Fun.

176
00:07:24,812 --> 00:07:26,780
And I've been around
an awful long time.

177
00:07:26,813 --> 00:07:27,582
Mm-hmm.

178
00:07:27,615 --> 00:07:28,715
Knew your father, I did.

179
00:07:28,748 --> 00:07:30,552
Hey, leave my
father out of this!

180
00:07:30,585 --> 00:07:31,785
And don't be
too sure I wasn't

181
00:07:31,818 --> 00:07:33,822
in the garden with
Mr. and Mrs. Adam.

182
00:07:33,855 --> 00:07:35,288
(shudders) You
were the snake.

183
00:07:35,322 --> 00:07:36,790
Why, you should know me.

184
00:07:36,823 --> 00:07:39,027
The spirit of music's
inside all of you.

185
00:07:39,060 --> 00:07:39,860
- Mm-hmm.
- No, I bathe.

186
00:07:39,893 --> 00:07:41,262
In you.

187
00:07:41,295 --> 00:07:42,997
In you.

188
00:07:43,030 --> 00:07:44,598
In all of you.

189
00:07:44,632 --> 00:07:49,237
Right there, waiting to be
found, waiting to be expressed.

190
00:07:49,270 --> 00:07:50,437
Waiting to die.

191
00:07:50,470 --> 00:07:51,805
All you've gotta
do is to want music.

192
00:07:51,838 --> 00:07:52,707
(robot laughs)

193
00:07:52,740 --> 00:07:54,675
- To want the fun of it.
- That's great.

194
00:07:54,708 --> 00:07:56,977
And when you
do, just call on me

195
00:07:57,010 --> 00:07:58,947
and I'll pop right
up to help you.

196
00:07:58,980 --> 00:08:00,313
No, that's okay.

197
00:08:00,347 --> 00:08:02,483
Generally, I live
in this music staff.

198
00:08:02,517 --> 00:08:04,585
But when I get a
message for help,

199
00:08:04,618 --> 00:08:07,688
I go off to appear
before people.

200
00:08:07,722 --> 00:08:11,125
Especially young people, when I
think I can do 'em some good.

201
00:08:11,158 --> 00:08:12,593
(robot laughs)
Oh, wonderful.

202
00:08:12,627 --> 00:08:14,728
Oh, I've known an awful
lot of people in my time.

203
00:08:14,762 --> 00:08:16,863
Got a whale of a lot of
swell stories to tell.

204
00:08:16,897 --> 00:08:18,165
I bet.

205
00:08:18,198 --> 00:08:20,802
One of the nicest is about
my good friend Buzz Turner,

206
00:08:20,835 --> 00:08:23,938
little guy I first met
when he was in junior high.

207
00:08:23,972 --> 00:08:26,807
Mr. B, you're hot.

208
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:28,242
- Wanna see what happened?
- No.

209
00:08:28,275 --> 00:08:30,545
Come on, let's go meet Buzz.

210
00:08:30,578 --> 00:08:31,378
That's okay.
(flute plays)

211
00:08:31,412 --> 00:08:33,447
(all scream)

212
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:35,783
Don't do that!

213
00:08:36,750 --> 00:08:38,152
(school bell rings)
I got a bad feeling.

214
00:08:38,185 --> 00:08:40,320
Did you ever notice the
excitement in a school corridor

215
00:08:40,353 --> 00:08:42,557
- at the end of the day?
- Oh yeah.

216
00:08:42,590 --> 00:08:45,025
More energy than you
can shake a stick at.

217
00:08:45,058 --> 00:08:47,762
The kind of energy that makes
fun and laughter and music.

218
00:08:47,795 --> 00:08:49,197
Hey, tone it down, lady.

219
00:08:49,230 --> 00:08:52,367
They're all there, with
all their young energy.

220
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:54,768
The first time I
really met Buzz Turner,

221
00:08:54,802 --> 00:08:57,905
including Jeanie, the
cutest girl in school.

222
00:08:57,938 --> 00:09:00,207
- I'll say.
- Including Buzz.

223
00:09:00,240 --> 00:09:02,610
- Nothing much wrong with Buzz.
- Except his hair.

224
00:09:02,643 --> 00:09:03,843
A good 12-year-old kid

225
00:09:03,877 --> 00:09:06,180
with a lot of normal
12-year-old problems.

226
00:09:06,213 --> 00:09:07,080
Buzz was a slacker.

227
00:09:07,113 --> 00:09:09,383
Just a little shy, maybe,

228
00:09:09,417 --> 00:09:11,285
wondering what it
takes to become part

229
00:09:11,318 --> 00:09:13,720
of the group, to really belong.

230
00:09:13,753 --> 00:09:18,092
- Conform. Conform.
- Conform. Conform.

231
00:09:18,125 --> 00:09:20,695
A kid who's searching
for the right answers.

232
00:09:20,728 --> 00:09:23,063
- It hurts a little.
- Hurts, don't it?

233
00:09:23,097 --> 00:09:24,732
How do you like
the new clarinet, Jim?

234
00:09:24,765 --> 00:09:26,500
- Hey.
- Oh, let's see, Fred.

235
00:09:26,533 --> 00:09:28,735
Got it from
the Franklin Mint.

236
00:09:28,768 --> 00:09:30,270
You should hear
the tone, wow.

237
00:09:30,303 --> 00:09:33,707
Hey Peg, wanna see the
step I was telling you about?

238
00:09:33,740 --> 00:09:35,743
- Oh sure.
- It's a goose-step.

239
00:09:35,777 --> 00:09:38,878
- Make some music, huh?
- All right, let's go.

240
00:09:38,912 --> 00:09:39,680
Eins, zwei.

241
00:09:39,713 --> 00:09:41,848
That's not how you play it.

242
00:09:41,882 --> 00:09:44,552
Eins, zwei. Oh,
to be like them.

243
00:09:44,585 --> 00:09:46,820
He's got a gun! Oh, no.

244
00:09:46,853 --> 00:09:49,757
(kids cheering)
Thought he had a gun there.

245
00:09:49,790 --> 00:09:50,992
Hey gang, tell you what.

246
00:09:51,025 --> 00:09:52,660
How about coming to
my house for a while?

247
00:09:52,693 --> 00:09:54,562
I got some great new records.

248
00:09:54,595 --> 00:09:55,797
Not me, Jeanie.

249
00:09:55,830 --> 00:09:58,132
Band practice, I wouldn't
miss it for anything.

250
00:09:58,165 --> 00:10:00,400
I'm buckin' for the
first clarinet chair.

251
00:10:00,433 --> 00:10:01,668
Oh, do you dare?

252
00:10:01,702 --> 00:10:03,403
Come on, Betty. Grab
your horn or we'll be late.

253
00:10:03,437 --> 00:10:06,707
- Okay, bye.
- Bye, so long.

254
00:10:06,740 --> 00:10:07,775
- They're a fun group.
- Yeah.

255
00:10:07,808 --> 00:10:09,443
These records
are the greatest.

256
00:10:09,477 --> 00:10:14,715
- Let's see now, we've got two.
- Wanna come? We can dance.

257
00:10:14,748 --> 00:10:16,483
- Don't hit me!
- No thanks.

258
00:10:16,517 --> 00:10:20,822
I don't know how, well, I
mean, I got some reading to do.

259
00:10:20,855 --> 00:10:22,423
You know, that
big history essay.

260
00:10:22,457 --> 00:10:24,792
But that's not
due for two weeks.

261
00:10:24,825 --> 00:10:27,828
I know Jeanie. Well,
I wanna get started.

262
00:10:27,862 --> 00:10:29,663
I gotta finish my
letter to Jodie Foster.

263
00:10:29,697 --> 00:10:34,668
- Well, come on then.
- What are we waiting for?

264
00:10:34,702 --> 00:10:36,703
That hurt. I'm
all messed up inside.

265
00:10:36,737 --> 00:10:41,208
If only an androgynous man
would come and visit me.

266
00:10:43,010 --> 00:10:48,582
I better wash my gym
clothes, it's been six months.

267
00:10:48,615 --> 00:10:52,520
Meanwhile, the Midvale
Police visit his locker.

268
00:10:52,553 --> 00:10:56,823
Find out why they call him Buzz.

269
00:10:56,857 --> 00:11:01,328
(trumpets sound)
(all) No!

270
00:11:01,362 --> 00:11:02,530
He's coming out of the closet.

271
00:11:02,530 --> 00:11:03,730
Looks like this boy
might be needing me.

272
00:11:03,730 --> 00:11:05,532
Well, that's what I'm here for.

273
00:11:05,565 --> 00:11:07,067
- I feel ill.
- Me too.

274
00:11:07,133 --> 00:11:09,437
Better wait 'til
he calls on me though,

275
00:11:09,470 --> 00:11:12,640
'til he reaches for the spirit.

276
00:11:12,673 --> 00:11:14,575
Calls for Satan (laughs).

277
00:11:14,642 --> 00:11:15,743
All right dear.

278
00:11:15,777 --> 00:11:17,978
Yeah, there's a weirdo
loose in the school.

279
00:11:18,045 --> 00:11:20,247
He was hiding in
Junior's locker.

280
00:11:20,247 --> 00:11:21,448
We've gotta call the police.

281
00:11:21,482 --> 00:11:23,750
Wait, I'm not, really, I
wouldn't want you to hang up.

282
00:11:23,750 --> 00:11:26,287
I'm not feeling--

283
00:11:26,353 --> 00:11:27,655
Nice house.

284
00:11:27,655 --> 00:11:30,157
Do they live in Pee-wee's
Playhouse? (laughs)

285
00:11:30,157 --> 00:11:34,495
- So early, dear?
- Yeah, I got some readin'.

286
00:11:34,562 --> 00:11:35,797
Well, it's such
a beautiful day,

287
00:11:35,863 --> 00:11:37,063
Daddy won't be home 'til six.

288
00:11:37,063 --> 00:11:39,067
I'm feeling very
sebaceous today, Mom.

289
00:11:39,100 --> 00:11:41,768
Better go up to my
room and do the reading.

290
00:11:41,768 --> 00:11:43,703
All right, son. Buzz?

291
00:11:43,770 --> 00:11:45,005
Don't make so much
noise when you read.

292
00:11:45,072 --> 00:11:46,673
Maybe we'll all go for
a nice ride after dinner

293
00:11:46,707 --> 00:11:49,177
- if Daddy's not too tired.
- Okay.

294
00:11:49,177 --> 00:11:54,682
Why does my kid have to
be such a dud? I was popular.

295
00:11:54,715 --> 00:11:57,585
(gasps)
Where'd you come from?

296
00:11:57,618 --> 00:11:58,785
Bellevue (laughs).

297
00:11:58,785 --> 00:12:01,088
Out there somewhere.
Do I look funny?

298
00:12:01,122 --> 00:12:02,088
You look hot.

299
00:12:02,088 --> 00:12:04,592
Well, it's kind
of a surprise.

300
00:12:04,625 --> 00:12:06,593
- I'll say.
- Mr. B what?

301
00:12:06,627 --> 00:12:09,197
Natural. Shouldn't
be surprised, boy.

302
00:12:09,230 --> 00:12:11,698
Whether you know it or
not, you sent for me.

303
00:12:11,732 --> 00:12:13,233
When you reached for
that music over there

304
00:12:13,267 --> 00:12:16,537
to make you feel better,
I got your message.

305
00:12:16,570 --> 00:12:17,638
You awakened me.

306
00:12:17,672 --> 00:12:18,838
So I'm attracted to guys now?

307
00:12:18,872 --> 00:12:20,073
You mean you've
been hiding here

308
00:12:20,107 --> 00:12:21,308
in my room all that time
and I never even noticed?

309
00:12:21,342 --> 00:12:23,743
You've seen me do everything?

310
00:12:23,777 --> 00:12:25,712
No boy, I don't mean that.

311
00:12:25,745 --> 00:12:29,783
I mean, you awakened the
spirit of music inside you.

312
00:12:29,817 --> 00:12:31,552
That's me. Your spirit of music.

313
00:12:31,585 --> 00:12:32,753
He played the
devil's tritone.

314
00:12:32,787 --> 00:12:36,157
That's my name,
boy, B Natural.

315
00:12:36,190 --> 00:12:37,057
Oh, mister lady.

316
00:12:37,090 --> 00:12:38,258
Why there's
nothing more natural

317
00:12:38,292 --> 00:12:40,962
than feelings of music
inside of people.

318
00:12:40,995 --> 00:12:44,732
Look here, boy. You sing a
baby a lullaby, and it coos.

319
00:12:44,765 --> 00:12:45,800
The lulla-baby-by.

320
00:12:45,833 --> 00:12:47,067
Expressing your
feelings with music

321
00:12:47,100 --> 00:12:49,837
is just as natural
as you can be.

322
00:12:49,870 --> 00:12:53,673
Gee, I don't know Mr. B,
I guess I like music okay.

323
00:12:53,707 --> 00:12:55,608
With the thing,
just please leave.

324
00:12:55,642 --> 00:12:57,878
But I don't know about
making music myself.

325
00:12:57,912 --> 00:13:00,547
I don't think I'd be any good.

326
00:13:00,580 --> 00:13:03,350
A boy talking
like that, blasphemy.

327
00:13:03,383 --> 00:13:05,018
Spanking time!

328
00:13:05,052 --> 00:13:07,220
I've been visiting with
an 8-year-old friend of mine

329
00:13:07,253 --> 00:13:09,290
- who woke me up recently.
- Oh God, no. No.

330
00:13:09,323 --> 00:13:11,725
Talked him into playing
the French horn, I did.

331
00:13:11,758 --> 00:13:14,795
You should hear him
(mimics French horn).

332
00:13:14,828 --> 00:13:16,763
He sucks, Mr. B Natural.

333
00:13:16,797 --> 00:13:19,667
Took to it like that,
because he wanted to.

334
00:13:19,700 --> 00:13:20,968
He wanted the fun of it.

335
00:13:21,002 --> 00:13:23,603
That's why he can play
that piece so easy.

336
00:13:23,637 --> 00:13:26,840
Wait'll he grows up and finds
out how hard it is. (laughs)

337
00:13:26,873 --> 00:13:29,643
That's very clever. Mom. Mom?

338
00:13:29,677 --> 00:13:33,380
Just because he's
young, he's not afraid.

339
00:13:33,413 --> 00:13:34,482
Wish I could.

340
00:13:34,515 --> 00:13:35,782
Wear tights
and prance around.

341
00:13:35,815 --> 00:13:40,053
Wish I could get in a band
or something and be as...

342
00:13:42,388 --> 00:13:43,857
well, be like Fred is.

343
00:13:43,890 --> 00:13:47,828
Now, none of that. None of
that. I'm here to help you,

344
00:13:47,862 --> 00:13:50,430
not to help you feel
sorry for yourself.

345
00:13:51,832 --> 00:13:53,833
- Ugh.
- Sure, you can be popular.

346
00:13:53,867 --> 00:13:55,335
- Sure, you can be in a band.
- Oh, Mr. B.

347
00:13:55,368 --> 00:13:57,738
And wait'll you see the kicks
you'll get out of it, Buzz.

348
00:13:57,772 --> 00:13:59,540
- Kicks.
- The glamour of the uniform,

349
00:13:59,573 --> 00:14:02,777
the thrill of traveling
for band competitions--

350
00:14:02,810 --> 00:14:04,845
The all-night coke
jags in cheap hotels.

351
00:14:04,878 --> 00:14:06,747
Just like being a
member of a football team,

352
00:14:06,780 --> 00:14:09,517
and most of all
Buzz, fun, fun, fun.

353
00:14:09,550 --> 00:14:12,720
That's nice. Mom!

354
00:14:12,753 --> 00:14:14,153
And I can do it for
you if you want me to,

355
00:14:14,187 --> 00:14:17,123
if you'll keep me
awake inside there.

356
00:14:17,157 --> 00:14:18,592
Bad touch.

357
00:14:19,860 --> 00:14:21,128
Are you a musician, Mr. B?

358
00:14:21,162 --> 00:14:22,362
Do you play any instruments?

359
00:14:22,395 --> 00:14:23,497
No, I'm in marketing.

360
00:14:23,530 --> 00:14:28,768
Me? They're members of my
family, boy. I love 'em all.

361
00:14:28,802 --> 00:14:32,472
Watch. Presto.

362
00:14:32,505 --> 00:14:34,742
- Allegro.
- Mom!

363
00:14:34,775 --> 00:14:37,343
Vivace.
(trumpets sound)

364
00:14:37,377 --> 00:14:38,245
Don't hurt me!

365
00:14:38,278 --> 00:14:39,813
Gosh, Mr. B, you
even talk music.

366
00:14:39,847 --> 00:14:40,982
You be bad.

367
00:14:41,015 --> 00:14:42,983
Anybody can,
Buzz. Be natural.

368
00:14:43,017 --> 00:14:45,552
Talk out what you
feel with music.

369
00:14:45,585 --> 00:14:48,722
Look here. Do you think
this is just a trumpet?

370
00:14:48,755 --> 00:14:49,957
- No.
- It's a bong!

371
00:14:49,990 --> 00:14:51,725
It's the feelings
that you want to feel,

372
00:14:51,758 --> 00:14:53,593
the things you want
to say to the world.

373
00:14:53,627 --> 00:14:56,663
Put a trumpet to your
lips and it becomes pride,

374
00:14:56,697 --> 00:14:59,867
confidence, the mood
of a happy king.

375
00:14:59,900 --> 00:15:04,405
(trumpet music)

376
00:15:04,438 --> 00:15:06,840
Oh my god, please say
this isn't happening.

377
00:15:06,873 --> 00:15:08,275
That's a happy king?

378
00:15:08,308 --> 00:15:13,513
Mr. B, shame on you. Have
you no sense of decency?

379
00:15:15,115 --> 00:15:19,787
A clarinet's not just a
clarinet, it's a happy smile!

380
00:15:19,820 --> 00:15:22,990
(clarinet music)

381
00:15:23,023 --> 00:15:27,628
(robots laugh)
He's so perky. Kill her.

382
00:15:29,630 --> 00:15:32,667
See, Buzz? It's really
fun to be psychotic (laughs).

383
00:15:32,700 --> 00:15:33,800
A trombone can let you

384
00:15:33,833 --> 00:15:37,403
show the world a
big laugh (laughs).

385
00:15:37,437 --> 00:15:40,140
(trombone music)

386
00:15:40,173 --> 00:15:42,042
Oh no.

387
00:15:43,443 --> 00:15:44,612
Why, Joel, why?

388
00:15:44,645 --> 00:15:48,582
When you want to
show dignity, Buzz,
try a French horn.

389
00:15:48,615 --> 00:15:49,717
(horn music)

390
00:15:49,750 --> 00:15:53,020
Mr. B, what would
you know about dignity?

391
00:15:53,053 --> 00:15:56,023
In tights and a hat.

392
00:15:56,057 --> 00:16:00,727
Buzz? When you want
to feel as big as giant,

393
00:16:00,760 --> 00:16:04,432
blow a sousaphone.
(sousaphone music)

394
00:16:04,465 --> 00:16:06,300
Now you're living in
your own fantasy world.

395
00:16:06,333 --> 00:16:09,403
We're into a whole
weird area here.

396
00:16:10,570 --> 00:16:11,605
Put 'em all together, Buzz,

397
00:16:11,638 --> 00:16:14,040
and you have any mood
you want to have fun.

398
00:16:14,073 --> 00:16:16,643
I'll talk, I'll
talk, don't hurt me!

399
00:16:16,677 --> 00:16:18,245
(marching band music)

400
00:16:18,278 --> 00:16:19,747
You know, I think Oscar Wilde

401
00:16:19,780 --> 00:16:22,348
only wished he was
this gay, you know?

402
00:16:22,382 --> 00:16:23,683
Ladies and
gentlemen, please accept

403
00:16:23,717 --> 00:16:27,353
our sincere apology
for all of this.

404
00:16:27,387 --> 00:16:28,622
Please.

405
00:16:29,590 --> 00:16:31,758
Forget music,
I want to dance!

406
00:16:31,792 --> 00:16:32,760
You like music?

407
00:16:32,793 --> 00:16:34,160
Sure sounds like fun,

408
00:16:34,193 --> 00:16:37,597
and I'd like to have some
of it, too, if I can.

409
00:16:37,630 --> 00:16:38,832
Don't touch me!

410
00:16:38,865 --> 00:16:39,900
That's the first big
step, boy, to want it.

411
00:16:39,933 --> 00:16:44,137
(new wave music plays)

412
00:16:46,207 --> 00:16:48,342
My boy was all
wound up, he was.

413
00:16:48,375 --> 00:16:49,208
Your boy?

414
00:16:49,242 --> 00:16:50,510
Told them all the
bandmaster told him

415
00:16:50,543 --> 00:16:52,545
how a boy could learn
discipline and coordination

416
00:16:52,578 --> 00:16:54,548
- and confidence through music.
- Oh.

417
00:16:54,582 --> 00:16:57,150
And make a personality grow.

418
00:16:57,183 --> 00:16:59,820
He said my face was
practically made for trumpet.

419
00:16:59,853 --> 00:17:01,555
I wanna learn, Dad.

420
00:17:01,588 --> 00:17:04,492
I wanna wear leotards and
a Peter Pan hat, Mom, Dad?

421
00:17:04,525 --> 00:17:07,460
Seems like you really
do a dance, son (laughs).

422
00:17:07,493 --> 00:17:09,095
If you don't think
there's a family

423
00:17:09,128 --> 00:17:11,265
- headed for the music store--
- I think it's David Bowie.

424
00:17:11,298 --> 00:17:12,967
then you don't know
parents who understand

425
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:16,203
what it takes to
make a child happy.

426
00:17:16,237 --> 00:17:17,838
You missed your
last payments, Pops.

427
00:17:17,872 --> 00:17:20,140
A half-dozen new
reeds for your saxophone.

428
00:17:20,173 --> 00:17:21,875
By the way, how are you doing

429
00:17:21,908 --> 00:17:23,743
in the fifth-grade
band in school?

430
00:17:23,777 --> 00:17:26,147
Oh, swell. I got first chair.

431
00:17:26,180 --> 00:17:28,482
Congratulations,
that's fine, young lady.

432
00:17:28,515 --> 00:17:29,850
- Goodbye.
- Bye.

433
00:17:29,883 --> 00:17:31,785
Don't patronize me, old man.

434
00:17:31,818 --> 00:17:32,820
Hello--

435
00:17:32,853 --> 00:17:34,053
We want to ask
you some questions.

436
00:17:34,087 --> 00:17:35,788
Yes, I believe so.
The boy here'd like

437
00:17:35,822 --> 00:17:37,223
to start on the trumpet.

438
00:17:37,257 --> 00:17:39,125
Well, you're joining a
mighty happy family, young man.

439
00:17:39,158 --> 00:17:40,327
I could use one.

440
00:17:40,360 --> 00:17:41,695
My son wouldn't like
my bringing this up,

441
00:17:41,728 --> 00:17:43,930
but I imagine a good
trumpet'd put quite a dent

442
00:17:43,963 --> 00:17:45,665
in his dad's checkbook, huh?

443
00:17:45,698 --> 00:17:46,600
Yeah, if he starts
hitting you with it (laughs).

444
00:17:46,633 --> 00:17:48,002
Not necessarily.

445
00:17:48,035 --> 00:17:50,903
All the better music dealers
have a trial purchase plan.

446
00:17:50,937 --> 00:17:53,573
You are simply making
a small investment

447
00:17:53,607 --> 00:17:55,675
in your son's
lifetime personality.

448
00:17:55,708 --> 00:17:57,577
Nah, put away the grease gun
and show me something cheap.

449
00:17:57,610 --> 00:18:00,480
And with this boy's
enthusiasm, he can't miss.

450
00:18:00,513 --> 00:18:02,115
All right, let's
look at some horns.

451
00:18:02,148 --> 00:18:04,117
Hey, this is the
plot from Golden Boy.

452
00:18:04,150 --> 00:18:05,685
Yeah, where's Bill Holden?

453
00:18:05,718 --> 00:18:08,088
This trumpet is made by the
world's largest manufacturer.

454
00:18:08,122 --> 00:18:09,523
Ah, cut to the
chase. How much?

455
00:18:09,557 --> 00:18:12,258
Oh, I don't think the
make is important, is it?

456
00:18:12,292 --> 00:18:13,227
Buzz?

457
00:18:13,260 --> 00:18:14,528
I don't think the
make matters much.

458
00:18:14,562 --> 00:18:16,963
(trumpet chirps)
Doesn't matter much?!

459
00:18:16,997 --> 00:18:18,865
Mom, Dad, tell
me you heard that?

460
00:18:18,898 --> 00:18:20,133
Thought I told you, boy.

461
00:18:20,167 --> 00:18:22,435
The most fun comes from
the most skilled playing.

462
00:18:22,468 --> 00:18:24,705
And the most skill comes
only from an instrument

463
00:18:24,738 --> 00:18:27,240
designed to give you
the best sound possible.

464
00:18:27,273 --> 00:18:29,142
It sure does matter.

465
00:18:29,175 --> 00:18:30,410
Oh, excuse me,
sexless man-woman.

466
00:18:30,443 --> 00:18:32,112
Why you should see
what care goes into

467
00:18:32,145 --> 00:18:34,047
making a really
superior instrument.

468
00:18:34,080 --> 00:18:36,250
You gotta inspect
your horn, boy.

469
00:18:36,283 --> 00:18:37,417
And wash it everyday.

470
00:18:37,450 --> 00:18:38,818
You oughta take
a look at a factory

471
00:18:38,852 --> 00:18:40,820
that makes top-quality
instruments.

472
00:18:40,853 --> 00:18:41,955
Oh, may I?

473
00:18:41,988 --> 00:18:43,157
Let me tell
you what goes into

474
00:18:43,190 --> 00:18:45,492
the making of the
best instruments.

475
00:18:45,525 --> 00:18:48,495
You see? The dealer
has the same idea.

476
00:18:48,528 --> 00:18:52,032
- You listen to him, Buzz.
- Or I'll kill you.

477
00:18:52,065 --> 00:18:53,600
The instrument
with the best sound

478
00:18:53,633 --> 00:18:56,437
comes out of plans that
combine modern equipment

479
00:18:56,470 --> 00:18:57,938
with precision craftsmanship.

480
00:18:57,972 --> 00:18:59,573
Oh cripes. Polish.
That's all I do all day.

481
00:18:59,607 --> 00:19:01,475
Hmm, no noise there.
How does this thing work?

482
00:19:01,508 --> 00:19:04,177
Superior workmanship
like this is possible

483
00:19:04,210 --> 00:19:06,880
because no one man
builds an instrument.

484
00:19:06,913 --> 00:19:08,182
These'll make
lovely noises someday.

485
00:19:08,215 --> 00:19:11,585
And each
operation's performed

486
00:19:11,618 --> 00:19:15,122
- by an expert in that phase.
- Work faster, Grandma.

487
00:19:15,155 --> 00:19:16,457
(tuba music)

488
00:19:16,490 --> 00:19:20,793
This man does things
he's not proud of.

489
00:19:20,827 --> 00:19:21,862
If that craftsman
stops spinning

490
00:19:21,895 --> 00:19:24,463
that sousaphone
bell for a second,

491
00:19:24,497 --> 00:19:27,500
he would completely change
the quality of the instrument.

492
00:19:27,533 --> 00:19:30,237
The faces of those he's
wronged float up at him.

493
00:19:30,270 --> 00:19:31,405
Hey, it's Leo Gorcey.
All grown up.

494
00:19:31,438 --> 00:19:33,373
A manufacturer of
quality instruments

495
00:19:33,407 --> 00:19:35,775
is equipped to produce
the best sound.

496
00:19:35,808 --> 00:19:39,345
He realizes that you play
the inside of an instrument,

497
00:19:39,378 --> 00:19:41,147
- not the outside.
- (laughs) Good point.

498
00:19:41,180 --> 00:19:45,152
So, he takes care to
produce a smooth finish inside

499
00:19:45,185 --> 00:19:47,420
that will not
obstruct sound through

500
00:19:47,453 --> 00:19:49,857
such methods as
hydraulic expansion.

501
00:19:49,890 --> 00:19:53,160
Oh, that feels good.

502
00:19:53,193 --> 00:19:55,228
The best instruments
are uniform.

503
00:19:55,262 --> 00:19:58,765
Every one is exactly like
every other of the same kind.

504
00:19:58,798 --> 00:19:59,700
Just like you and me.

505
00:19:59,733 --> 00:20:00,900
Valves must be ground

506
00:20:00,933 --> 00:20:04,170
with utmost accuracy for
smooth, easy playing.

507
00:20:04,203 --> 00:20:06,707
James Joyce.

508
00:20:06,740 --> 00:20:08,808
Out of this unseemly,
unfinished heap--

509
00:20:08,842 --> 00:20:09,843
Honey West!

510
00:20:09,877 --> 00:20:11,912
with the skill of experience,

511
00:20:11,945 --> 00:20:13,313
(robot gasps)

512
00:20:13,347 --> 00:20:16,850
Will come a row of gleaming,
high-quality saxophones.

513
00:20:16,883 --> 00:20:18,485
Come on now,
watch the red man.

514
00:20:18,518 --> 00:20:20,320
There is the high skill of
craftsmanship, for instance,

515
00:20:20,353 --> 00:20:22,588
that produces a seamless bell.

516
00:20:22,622 --> 00:20:24,925
Further removing
sound resistance,

517
00:20:24,958 --> 00:20:29,328
giving to the finished Coprion
instrument an ease of playing

518
00:20:29,362 --> 00:20:32,765
and a tone quality
that is unmatched.

519
00:20:32,798 --> 00:20:34,968
Extra value is what you
get when you play the coronet.

520
00:20:35,002 --> 00:20:36,303
One manufacturer develops

521
00:20:36,337 --> 00:20:38,438
the highest-quality instruments.

522
00:20:38,472 --> 00:20:40,373
This trumpet is flatlining.

523
00:20:40,407 --> 00:20:43,377
Tone testing ensures
their best performance.

524
00:20:43,410 --> 00:20:45,878
- Testing to analyze the tone--
- In a gas chamber?

525
00:20:45,912 --> 00:20:48,248
to measure the
sound or accuracy

526
00:20:48,282 --> 00:20:52,085
to a tiny fraction
of a semitone.

527
00:20:52,118 --> 00:20:53,987
Oh cripes, we
gotta work faster.

528
00:20:54,020 --> 00:20:55,122
Hurry up Margie.

529
00:20:55,155 --> 00:20:56,557
Oh, we got a long way to go.

530
00:20:56,590 --> 00:20:59,927
He got so many of these
stories, they're just so shiny.

531
00:20:59,960 --> 00:21:01,528
Let's break.

532
00:21:01,562 --> 00:21:03,063
This instrument will make
music fun for you, young fella.

533
00:21:03,097 --> 00:21:06,132
It was handmade
by an old woman.

534
00:21:06,165 --> 00:21:08,202
Good luck and I'll
see you in the band.

535
00:21:08,235 --> 00:21:10,737
Wait a minute, I
didn't say I'd take it.

536
00:21:12,172 --> 00:21:13,173
- Well--
- We bilked 'em.

537
00:21:13,207 --> 00:21:15,042
he's really on his way now.

538
00:21:15,075 --> 00:21:16,777
I had done my part.

539
00:21:16,810 --> 00:21:19,980
The spirit of music was a
natural thing with him now.

540
00:21:20,013 --> 00:21:21,615
For the rest of the story,

541
00:21:21,648 --> 00:21:24,217
I could just sit back
and watch the training.

542
00:21:24,250 --> 00:21:25,585
And rake in the cash.

543
00:21:25,618 --> 00:21:28,522
Watch him open
up as a new person.

544
00:21:29,757 --> 00:21:33,993
Aw, no, Jerry, you blow in
the big end. Yeah, like that.

545
00:21:34,027 --> 00:21:35,462
Good, am I hip yet?

546
00:21:35,495 --> 00:21:38,598
When do the chicks
start coming around?

547
00:21:38,632 --> 00:21:40,033
In the months that
led to graduation,

548
00:21:40,067 --> 00:21:43,737
all the things happened to
Buzz that we knew would happen.

549
00:21:43,770 --> 00:21:46,038
He was ready to
really belong now.

550
00:21:46,072 --> 00:21:47,507
But the rules had changed.

551
00:21:47,540 --> 00:21:48,642
By the time he
finished high school,

552
00:21:48,675 --> 00:21:50,977
he would be ready to
take that big solo.

553
00:21:51,010 --> 00:21:54,147
He'd jam with his
imaginary friend.

554
00:21:54,180 --> 00:21:55,648
(upbeat trumpet music)

555
00:21:55,682 --> 00:21:59,787
Now the poor kid grandstands
at every opportunity.

556
00:21:59,820 --> 00:22:01,922
Hmm, sucks, doesn't he?

557
00:22:04,523 --> 00:22:06,727
If you get near
a song, play it.

558
00:22:07,793 --> 00:22:09,930
Enough. What is this,
Flight of the Bobblehead?

559
00:22:09,963 --> 00:22:11,732
Come on. Stop!

560
00:22:11,765 --> 00:22:14,467
Sorry, sorry. Thought they
were trading fours there.

561
00:22:14,500 --> 00:22:16,202
(slow waltz music)

562
00:22:16,235 --> 00:22:17,637
[all sing]
Really, really white.

563
00:22:17,670 --> 00:22:22,308
We're really, really,
really, really white.

564
00:22:22,342 --> 00:22:27,547
White, white, white. We're
really, really white.

565
00:22:28,648 --> 00:22:31,283
Oh, it's Satchmo!
What do you know?

566
00:22:31,317 --> 00:22:32,485
(trumpet plays)

567
00:22:32,518 --> 00:22:35,688
(robot mimics trumpet)

568
00:22:35,722 --> 00:22:38,258
Say, that guy's got
a way with a ballad.

569
00:22:38,292 --> 00:22:40,627
Let me tell ya.

570
00:22:40,660 --> 00:22:44,063
Well, the old clock on the
wall says that it's that's all

571
00:22:44,097 --> 00:22:47,200
from the Stridex
Medicated Band Hour.

572
00:22:47,233 --> 00:22:50,503
Sounds a little like
Leigh Kamman there.

573
00:22:50,537 --> 00:22:53,307
This was before his
collaboration with Gil Evans.

574
00:22:53,340 --> 00:22:57,110
Thank you, thank you.
(applause)

575
00:22:57,143 --> 00:22:59,712
It stinks! (laughs)

576
00:23:01,180 --> 00:23:02,748
Ah, but in real life,

577
00:23:02,782 --> 00:23:06,620
Johnny is last chair
with the preschool band.

578
00:23:06,653 --> 00:23:09,890
No one expresses more
naturally than a child.

579
00:23:09,923 --> 00:23:12,858
And there is no more natural
expression than music.

580
00:23:12,892 --> 00:23:13,827
Let's get outta here.

581
00:23:13,860 --> 00:23:15,595
Now, is Mr. B Natural
a man or a woman?

582
00:23:15,628 --> 00:23:17,697
- Oh, definitely a man.
- Oh, no, no, no.

583
00:23:17,730 --> 00:23:19,198
No, a man, definitely a man.

584
00:23:19,232 --> 00:23:20,400
- Nuh-uh.
- Mm-hmm.

585
00:23:20,433 --> 00:23:23,970
(marching band music)

586
00:23:24,003 --> 00:23:28,508
(doors closing hydraulically)

587
00:23:32,378 --> 00:23:34,080
It's a woman,
a she, a missus.

588
00:23:34,113 --> 00:23:35,015
No, no, no, turn it off!

589
00:23:35,048 --> 00:23:35,948
It's a Sheila.

590
00:23:35,982 --> 00:23:37,683
Hey, you guys. Take it easy.

591
00:23:37,717 --> 00:23:39,218
You're never gonna
get anywhere that way.

592
00:23:39,252 --> 00:23:40,620
Let's just get organized.

593
00:23:40,653 --> 00:23:42,855
I'll give you both two
minutes to state your case

594
00:23:42,888 --> 00:23:46,025
and then time for a
short rebuttal, okay?

595
00:23:46,058 --> 00:23:47,627
- (both) OK.
- All right.

596
00:23:47,660 --> 00:23:51,030
Now, standard debate rules.
I will act as arbiter.

597
00:23:51,063 --> 00:23:52,965
On my mark, begin. Mr. Crow?

598
00:23:52,998 --> 00:23:55,502
Mr. B Natural was a woman.

599
00:23:55,535 --> 00:23:57,937
Of this, there can be no doubt.

600
00:23:57,970 --> 00:23:59,005
Not a chance, fat-butt.

601
00:23:59,038 --> 00:24:01,542
Hey, hey. That's one
penalty point for Mr. Servo.

602
00:24:01,575 --> 00:24:02,942
Mr. Crow, begin again.

603
00:24:02,975 --> 00:24:04,443
Thank you, commissioner.

604
00:24:04,477 --> 00:24:06,680
We are compelled to look
on the mister moniker

605
00:24:06,713 --> 00:24:10,383
as merely a whimsical
contrived characterization

606
00:24:10,417 --> 00:24:11,818
in the spirit of Peter Pan,

607
00:24:11,852 --> 00:24:14,353
played convincingly by
the late Mary Martin,

608
00:24:14,387 --> 00:24:15,555
and the late Cathy Rigby,

609
00:24:15,588 --> 00:24:16,490
and the late Sandy
Duncan, and the late--

610
00:24:16,523 --> 00:24:18,458
Get to your point, Mr. Robot!

611
00:24:18,492 --> 00:24:20,660
- The point sir, is breasts.
- Huh?

612
00:24:20,693 --> 00:24:22,528
Yes, Mr. B
Natural had breasts.

613
00:24:22,562 --> 00:24:24,630
A decidedly unnatural
thing for a man to have,

614
00:24:24,663 --> 00:24:26,332
don't you think, Mr. Servo?

615
00:24:26,365 --> 00:24:29,635
When one takes into
account the short jacket,

616
00:24:29,668 --> 00:24:31,772
the shiny leotard,
the wide hips,

617
00:24:31,805 --> 00:24:35,442
unless one is wildly confused,
as my colleague seems to be,

618
00:24:35,475 --> 00:24:37,910
one concludes, naturally,
that Mr. B Natural

619
00:24:37,943 --> 00:24:42,648
is indeed a wonderful, spritely,
albeit annoying, woman.

620
00:24:42,682 --> 00:24:45,318
Thank you Mr. T. Robot.
Your response, Mr. Servo?

621
00:24:45,352 --> 00:24:46,820
Oh, thank you Mr. Utley.

622
00:24:46,853 --> 00:24:49,388
Ignoring the hermetic
contumely hurled at me

623
00:24:49,422 --> 00:24:51,223
by my less-than-erudite
conferee,

624
00:24:51,257 --> 00:24:52,792
no doubt placed as a thin veil

625
00:24:52,825 --> 00:24:54,660
before an atrabilious
temperment--

626
00:24:54,693 --> 00:24:55,795
Mr. Servo, I'm warning you,

627
00:24:55,828 --> 00:24:57,497
no William F. Buckley
impersonations

628
00:24:57,530 --> 00:24:59,065
under the threat of
a point reduction.

629
00:24:59,098 --> 00:25:00,600
Okay, sorry.

630
00:25:00,633 --> 00:25:02,202
Mr. B Natural, what a guy.

631
00:25:02,235 --> 00:25:05,005
Yes, Mr. B Natural is
a decidedly modern man.

632
00:25:05,038 --> 00:25:07,040
Ignoring the constrictions
placed upon him

633
00:25:07,073 --> 00:25:10,977
by modern society, Mr. B
Natural dresses as he does,

634
00:25:11,010 --> 00:25:12,178
just as he sees fit.

635
00:25:12,212 --> 00:25:13,847
If this means dressing
in a shimmering leotard

636
00:25:13,880 --> 00:25:15,615
and a powder-blue,
note-spangled jacket,

637
00:25:15,648 --> 00:25:16,783
and climbing through the windows

638
00:25:16,817 --> 00:25:18,017
