744
00:29:15,655 --> 00:29:17,523
That's a hard
question to answer.

745
00:29:17,557 --> 00:29:18,725
Look, here's what happened.

746
00:29:18,758 --> 00:29:20,025
The season opens next week and

747
00:29:20,058 --> 00:29:21,760
I'm expecting a crowd in town.

748
00:29:21,795 --> 00:29:22,762
So you were expec-- (laughs)

749
00:29:22,795 --> 00:29:24,230
So I was stocking
up on supplies,

750
00:29:24,263 --> 00:29:25,965
bringing this truckload of
groceries down from Calexico.

751
00:29:25,998 --> 00:29:26,765
- What kind of--
- Get the picture?

752
00:29:26,800 --> 00:29:27,967
Uh, yes. But--

753
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:29,168
Well, I'd left the
club station wagon

754
00:29:29,202 --> 00:29:31,070
in San Felipe, get a
new rear end put in it.

755
00:29:31,103 --> 00:29:32,605
Oh, did you ever think of a--

756
00:29:32,638 --> 00:29:34,073
So when I got there, I had
to stop and pick that up too.

757
00:29:34,107 --> 00:29:35,040
Get the picture?

758
00:29:35,073 --> 00:29:36,775
Now, I couldn't run two cars,

759
00:29:36,810 --> 00:29:39,145
so I hired this kid
to drive the truck.

760
00:29:39,178 --> 00:29:42,015
Started him out a couple,
three hours ahead of me.

761
00:29:42,048 --> 00:29:43,248
Yeah?

762
00:29:43,282 --> 00:29:44,383
Followed him in the station
wagon. Get the picture?

763
00:29:44,417 --> 00:29:45,518
Oh sure I do, but it was--

764
00:29:45,552 --> 00:29:46,752
Well, he was supposed
to meet me here.

765
00:29:46,785 --> 00:29:48,722
He hasn't arrived yet
and that was yesterday.

766
00:29:48,755 --> 00:29:49,855
Well that would
mean he's in--

767
00:29:49,888 --> 00:29:51,723
- Have you looked for him?
- Everywhere.

768
00:29:51,757 --> 00:29:53,392
Then he must have
gone back to the club.

769
00:29:53,425 --> 00:29:55,228
Well, I just came from there.

770
00:29:55,262 --> 00:29:56,695
Then you must
have passed him up

771
00:29:56,728 --> 00:29:58,965
somewhere on the
road from San Felipe.

772
00:29:58,998 --> 00:30:00,132
If I passed him on the road,

773
00:30:00,167 --> 00:30:01,600
don't you think I'd
know my own truck?

774
00:30:01,633 --> 00:30:02,602
Get your greasy
white paws off my desk.

775
00:30:02,635 --> 00:30:03,703
I'm sure you would, señor.

776
00:30:03,737 --> 00:30:05,172
But, what if it
went off the road?

777
00:30:05,205 --> 00:30:06,772
There's no place he
can pull off the road

778
00:30:06,805 --> 00:30:07,973
without getting
stuck in the sand!

779
00:30:08,007 --> 00:30:10,008
And how could
your truck be lost?

780
00:30:10,042 --> 00:30:12,345
If it wasn't lost,
would I be coming to you?

781
00:30:12,378 --> 00:30:13,112
[all] Third base!

782
00:30:13,145 --> 00:30:14,780
That is a good
question señor.

783
00:30:14,813 --> 00:30:19,352
Tell me about this boy that was
driving it. What's his name?

784
00:30:19,385 --> 00:30:22,322
Miguel something or other.

785
00:30:22,355 --> 00:30:26,658
Probably 15, 16 years
old, dark, thin,

786
00:30:26,692 --> 00:30:28,227
about, oh about so high.

787
00:30:28,260 --> 00:30:29,695
Well señor, you
just described me.

788
00:30:29,728 --> 00:30:31,097
Would you know
him if you saw him?

789
00:30:31,130 --> 00:30:32,098
Yes!

790
00:30:32,132 --> 00:30:33,533
Come across the
street with me señor.

791
00:30:33,567 --> 00:30:35,502
We'll buy you a new boy.

792
00:30:38,037 --> 00:30:40,673
Pat Boone's going
through the liquor cabinet.

793
00:30:40,707 --> 00:30:43,843
This is stupid,
there's no F in morphine.

794
00:30:43,877 --> 00:30:47,380
Now let's see here,
little crystal methedrine,

795
00:30:47,413 --> 00:30:48,380
and that's some monoxo--

796
00:30:48,413 --> 00:30:51,783
Oh no, I was just
cleaning, just dusting.

797
00:30:51,817 --> 00:30:54,620
That's him.
Where's my truck?!

798
00:30:54,653 --> 00:30:55,888
- Here, let me.
- Uno momento.

799
00:30:55,922 --> 00:30:57,723
- What's a matter with him?
- Where's his truck?

800
00:30:57,757 --> 00:31:02,128
(speaking in Spanish)

801
00:31:05,298 --> 00:31:06,532
Did you get that?

802
00:31:06,565 --> 00:31:08,400
Miguel is a very sick boy.
He's suffering from shock.

803
00:31:08,433 --> 00:31:09,735
What from?

804
00:31:09,768 --> 00:31:13,205
(speaking in Spanish)

805
00:31:13,238 --> 00:31:14,073
He said something
shocked him.

806
00:31:14,107 --> 00:31:15,508
Nobody seems to know.

807
00:31:15,542 --> 00:31:17,410
What did you
do with my truck?!

808
00:31:17,443 --> 00:31:19,812
Here, let me. What did
you do with his truck?!

809
00:31:19,845 --> 00:31:24,250
(speaking in Spanish)

810
00:31:28,487 --> 00:31:31,490
Quiet, I'm seeing Bernadette.

811
00:31:31,523 --> 00:31:36,162
Yes? Yes, yes? He says,
"Get your elbow off my chest."

812
00:31:37,797 --> 00:31:40,433
Beware the dwarf.

813
00:31:41,467 --> 00:31:42,735
Halitosis.

814
00:31:42,768 --> 00:31:45,905
(speaking in Spanish)

815
00:31:45,938 --> 00:31:48,373
- What'd he say?
- It's no use.

816
00:31:48,407 --> 00:31:50,843
If he gets any better, the
doctor will let us know.

817
00:31:50,877 --> 00:31:51,945
Come with me, I will take you

818
00:31:51,978 --> 00:31:53,178
to the place where he was found.

819
00:31:53,212 --> 00:31:54,413
We could pump his stomach,

820
00:31:54,447 --> 00:31:57,483
but I don't think
we'd find your truck.

821
00:31:57,517 --> 00:31:58,952
Will young Miguel be cured?

822
00:31:58,985 --> 00:32:02,488
And what about the pick-up
truck? Tune in tomorrow.

823
00:32:02,522 --> 00:32:05,492
Yes, you'll drive to the
mutated monster scenes in style

824
00:32:05,525 --> 00:32:07,793
in the new 1953 Desoto.

825
00:32:07,827 --> 00:32:11,663
(ominous orchestra music)

826
00:32:13,032 --> 00:32:15,535
Would you like to have
lunch with me here, señor?

827
00:32:15,568 --> 00:32:17,170
A little pic-a-nic?

828
00:32:17,203 --> 00:32:19,605
He asked me. He asked me.

829
00:32:21,140 --> 00:32:25,010
Well, it looks like
a ton of chili to me.

830
00:32:25,043 --> 00:32:27,813
Was it your truck
made these tracks?

831
00:32:27,847 --> 00:32:29,482
No, it was beauty
killed the beast.

832
00:32:29,515 --> 00:32:33,853
Must have. I had two new
retreads like that on the rear.

833
00:32:33,887 --> 00:32:36,655
Came down the
road and got stuck.

834
00:32:36,688 --> 00:32:38,357
Yeah. Then where'd it go?

835
00:32:38,390 --> 00:32:39,958
Straight up into
the air, it looks like.

836
00:32:39,992 --> 00:32:41,127
Look, that truck had a radio

837
00:32:41,160 --> 00:32:42,928
and a heater, but it
didn't have wings.

838
00:32:42,962 --> 00:32:44,163
What's the Spanish
word for bite me?

839
00:32:44,197 --> 00:32:45,432
Now are you gonna
find it or aren't you?

840
00:32:45,465 --> 00:32:48,233
Look señor. I am just
a country policeman.

841
00:32:48,267 --> 00:32:50,068
I am paid to keep the peace.

842
00:32:50,102 --> 00:32:52,072
If someone steals, I
try to catch the thief

843
00:32:52,105 --> 00:32:55,273
and put 'em in jail,
like it says in the book.

844
00:32:55,307 --> 00:32:58,643
But about this
señor, I don't know.

845
00:32:58,678 --> 00:33:01,347
The book doesn't say anything
about a thing like this.

846
00:33:01,380 --> 00:33:04,617
Well, we're writing
the book right now, pal.

847
00:33:04,650 --> 00:33:07,953
Hey, let's drag Clarence
Thomas' name through there.

848
00:33:07,987 --> 00:33:09,655
Noise pollution.

849
00:33:09,688 --> 00:33:13,425
By 1970, this could be our
nation's number-one problem.

850
00:33:13,458 --> 00:33:14,960
Guests of the
amazing, Colossal Beast

851
00:33:14,993 --> 00:33:17,163
stay at the Beverly Hilton.

852
00:33:19,398 --> 00:33:22,835
And that's the latest report
on the international scene.

853
00:33:22,868 --> 00:33:24,203
Now, on the lighter
side of the news.

854
00:33:24,237 --> 00:33:25,372
There was a young
man from Nantucket.

855
00:33:25,405 --> 00:33:26,705
A dispatch from
Guavas, Mexico,

856
00:33:26,738 --> 00:33:28,975
says that Mr. John Swanson
is having a little trouble

857
00:33:29,008 --> 00:33:31,443
collecting insurance
on his stolen truck.

858
00:33:31,477 --> 00:33:32,778
- What happened to it?
- Oh, she's a social drinker.

859
00:33:32,812 --> 00:33:33,947
Well according
to his claim report,

860
00:33:33,980 --> 00:33:37,250
it disappeared without
leaving any tracks.

861
00:33:37,283 --> 00:33:41,187
Mr. Swanson says something
must've carried off his truck.

862
00:33:41,220 --> 00:33:43,757
Now, what could have
carried off his truck?

863
00:33:43,790 --> 00:33:45,525
Ha-ha, that's whimsical.

864
00:33:45,558 --> 00:33:47,960
And here in Los Angeles,
the city council has decided

865
00:33:47,993 --> 00:33:49,162
to vote upon the referendum

866
00:33:49,195 --> 00:33:52,030
for increasing the
pay of teachers.

867
00:33:52,065 --> 00:33:53,532
With the interest that
has recently been aroused

868
00:33:53,565 --> 00:33:54,667
regarding the importance
of better schools--

869
00:33:54,700 --> 00:33:55,968
Hello, I'm out of tonic.

870
00:33:56,002 --> 00:33:59,472
Operator, I want to place
a person-to-person call.

871
00:33:59,505 --> 00:34:02,508
To Mr. John Swanson
in Guavos, Mexico.

872
00:34:02,542 --> 00:34:03,675
I'll hold the line.

873
00:34:03,708 --> 00:34:05,077
Uh, you are holding the line.

874
00:34:05,110 --> 00:34:06,478
I'm getting me a good lawyer.

875
00:34:06,512 --> 00:34:08,447
And if one ain't
enough, I'm getting two.

876
00:34:08,480 --> 00:34:09,782
And I'm gonna make
the insurance company

877
00:34:09,815 --> 00:34:11,283
pay up and pay up fast.

878
00:34:11,317 --> 00:34:12,418
I'm taking 'em to court by the--

879
00:34:12,452 --> 00:34:14,020
Oh, who am I
kidding? I love you.

880
00:34:14,053 --> 00:34:17,657
I'm sorry, I guess I
shouldn't blow my top like that.

881
00:34:17,690 --> 00:34:18,825
Uh, you don't have a top.

882
00:34:18,858 --> 00:34:19,658
But if somebody
calls me a liar, I--

883
00:34:19,692 --> 00:34:20,727
(doorbell rings)

884
00:34:20,760 --> 00:34:21,593
Sorry, time's up.
Thanks for playing.

885
00:34:21,627 --> 00:34:22,060
Excuse me.

886
00:34:22,095 --> 00:34:26,332
(new wave music)

887
00:34:31,970 --> 00:34:33,338
Joyce Manning?

888
00:34:33,372 --> 00:34:35,642
Yes, you must be Major
Baird. Please Come in.

889
00:34:35,675 --> 00:34:37,377
Thank you.

890
00:34:37,410 --> 00:34:39,012
Who's the stiff?

891
00:34:39,045 --> 00:34:40,880
This is Mr.
Swanson. Major Baird.

892
00:34:40,913 --> 00:34:41,947
- How do you do, sir?
- Howdy.

893
00:34:41,980 --> 00:34:43,048
Hit the road, Pops.

894
00:34:43,082 --> 00:34:44,983
- Please be seated.
- Thank you.

895
00:34:45,017 --> 00:34:46,385
Would you like a drink?

896
00:34:46,418 --> 00:34:48,387
No thanks.

897
00:34:48,420 --> 00:34:50,088
Mr. Swanson was good
enough to come up here

898
00:34:50,122 --> 00:34:51,990
and tell me of his experience.

899
00:34:52,023 --> 00:34:53,392
It was a mere nothing.

900
00:34:53,425 --> 00:34:54,927
I had to be in Los
Angeles anyway today

901
00:34:54,960 --> 00:34:56,695
to see the insurance people.

902
00:34:56,728 --> 00:34:57,763
Have I missed much?

903
00:34:57,797 --> 00:34:58,865
Well, he tells me
that he wasn't there

904
00:34:58,898 --> 00:35:00,398
to see what happened
to his truck.

905
00:35:00,432 --> 00:35:03,368
I guess I got
there just too late.

906
00:35:03,402 --> 00:35:04,470
Oh, I sense the irony.

907
00:35:04,503 --> 00:35:06,038
Besides the truck's
not being there,

908
00:35:06,072 --> 00:35:08,507
did you see anything unusual.

909
00:35:08,540 --> 00:35:11,177
- Like what?
- Tracks in the sand.

910
00:35:11,210 --> 00:35:13,078
Well, there were
car tracks, sure.

911
00:35:13,112 --> 00:35:15,047
But no animal tracks?

912
00:35:15,080 --> 00:35:16,348
Animal tracks?

913
00:35:16,382 --> 00:35:18,885
Human footprints,
Mr. Swanson.

914
00:35:18,952 --> 00:35:20,452
Well what's so
unusual about that?

915
00:35:20,487 --> 00:35:23,655
These would be the tracks
of a big man, a very big man.

916
00:35:23,688 --> 00:35:24,857
We're talkin' big.

917
00:35:24,857 --> 00:35:28,060
There were Miguel's.
I don't get the picture.

918
00:35:28,093 --> 00:35:29,762
- How big do you mean?
- Very big.

919
00:35:29,762 --> 00:35:30,663
Dom DeLuise big?

920
00:35:30,663 --> 00:35:32,298
10 times as big as you.

921
00:35:32,365 --> 00:35:34,067
Are you trying to kid me?

922
00:35:34,067 --> 00:35:35,702
Hey, I'm drunk,
but not that drunk!

923
00:35:35,768 --> 00:35:37,570
Not at all. I'm trying
to find my brother.

924
00:35:37,570 --> 00:35:39,305
He got a bad dose
of radiation poisoning

925
00:35:39,372 --> 00:35:41,673
in the course of testing
a plutonium bomb.

926
00:35:41,673 --> 00:35:44,877
He started growing at the rate
of eight to ten feet a day.

927
00:35:44,910 --> 00:35:45,878
He blew his
mind out in a car.

928
00:35:45,878 --> 00:35:47,280
You may have read about him.

929
00:35:47,280 --> 00:35:50,683
Sure, Colonel Glenn
Manning, the Colossal Man.

930
00:35:50,683 --> 00:35:51,717
I remember.

931
00:35:51,783 --> 00:35:52,885
Glenn had reached a height

932
00:35:52,885 --> 00:35:54,887
of over 60 feet
when he disappeared.

933
00:35:54,920 --> 00:35:55,888
I'll have what she's having.

934
00:35:55,888 --> 00:35:57,022
Didn't he get
shot and fall off

935
00:35:57,088 --> 00:35:58,523
of Boulder Dam and get killed?

936
00:35:58,590 --> 00:36:01,193
That's right. But Miss
Manning seems to have an idea

937
00:36:01,227 --> 00:36:04,030
that he survived both the
artillery fire and the fall.

938
00:36:04,097 --> 00:36:05,297
You see, she lives in denial.

939
00:36:05,330 --> 00:36:06,898
That is what you're
getting at, isn't it?

940
00:36:06,898 --> 00:36:08,633
I'll leave it
up to Mr. Swanson.

941
00:36:08,700 --> 00:36:11,237
Wouldn't that account for the
disappearance of your truck?

942
00:36:11,303 --> 00:36:13,605
Look lady, leave
me out of this.

943
00:36:13,605 --> 00:36:16,742
I didn't see no footprints.
I didn't see no giant.

944
00:36:16,808 --> 00:36:18,010
I didn't see anything.

945
00:36:18,043 --> 00:36:20,847
I've got enough trouble with
the insurance company as it is.

946
00:36:20,913 --> 00:36:21,913
Get the picture?

947
00:36:21,947 --> 00:36:23,748
I've had enough
hurt in my life.

948
00:36:23,815 --> 00:36:26,652
Can you think of anyone
else who might help me?

949
00:36:26,718 --> 00:36:28,320
There's a boy named
Miguel down there,

950
00:36:28,353 --> 00:36:30,122
but he's not doing any talking.

951
00:36:30,155 --> 00:36:32,658
Doctor said he's suffering
from shock or something.

952
00:36:32,692 --> 00:36:35,628
I'm sorry I can't do you
more good Miss Manning.

953
00:36:35,662 --> 00:36:37,530
Thank you for
coming, Mr. Swanson.

954
00:36:37,563 --> 00:36:40,365
Oh say, can I have a
to-go cup for that gin?

955
00:36:42,402 --> 00:36:44,803
Hadn't you better give
him up, Miss Manning?

956
00:36:44,837 --> 00:36:46,172
The Army did some time ago.

957
00:36:46,205 --> 00:36:47,540
You never found his body.

958
00:36:47,573 --> 00:36:50,475
The river below the dam is
a mile deep in some places.

959
00:36:50,508 --> 00:36:54,513
His body's down there somewhere
and it'll show up in time.

960
00:36:54,547 --> 00:36:57,350
You think Glenn made his
way down that river somehow

961
00:36:57,383 --> 00:36:59,118
and reached the Gulf of
California, don't you?

962
00:36:59,152 --> 00:37:00,052
The whole sequel
depends on it!

963
00:37:00,085 --> 00:37:01,687
Believe me, it's impossible.

964
00:37:01,720 --> 00:37:04,490
I was in charge of the
search for him and I know.

965
00:37:04,523 --> 00:37:05,625
The medical
authorities all agree.

966
00:37:05,658 --> 00:37:07,493
No man, no matter
what his size--

967
00:37:07,527 --> 00:37:08,493
Is an island?

968
00:37:08,527 --> 00:37:09,828
could take those
two bazooka charges

969
00:37:09,862 --> 00:37:11,263
and a drop of over 700 feet--

970
00:37:11,297 --> 00:37:12,398
And not want it every night.

971
00:37:12,432 --> 00:37:14,833
and come through it alive.

972
00:37:14,867 --> 00:37:16,502
Well, those are the facts.

973
00:37:16,535 --> 00:37:19,838
Your brother died a
watery death. Well, gotta go.

974
00:37:19,872 --> 00:37:21,440
Take my advice and face them.

975
00:37:21,473 --> 00:37:24,710
I shall, Major.
Thank you for coming.

976
00:37:24,743 --> 00:37:26,812
I'm afraid it wasn't
worth your while.

977
00:37:26,845 --> 00:37:28,913
It could be if you'd have
dinner with me this evening.

978
00:37:28,948 --> 00:37:29,815
Woah, down boy!

979
00:37:29,848 --> 00:37:31,317
I'd like to, but I can't.

980
00:37:31,350 --> 00:37:33,852
I'm leaving for
Mexico right away.

981
00:37:33,885 --> 00:37:35,087
- Goodbye.
- Oh man, I blew it.

982
00:37:35,120 --> 00:37:38,523
- Goodbye.
- Stupid, stupid, stupid.

983
00:37:38,557 --> 00:37:40,425
What a dink.

984
00:37:42,428 --> 00:37:45,898
Life is kind of different
in Grover's Corneros.

985
00:37:45,932 --> 00:37:46,798
(slow Latin music)

986
00:37:46,832 --> 00:37:48,133
It could be worth my while

987
00:37:48,167 --> 00:37:50,568
if you had dinner
with me tonight.

988
00:37:51,903 --> 00:37:53,805
Oh, just snap out of it.

989
00:37:53,838 --> 00:37:55,808
That is the boy, Miguel.

990
00:37:55,842 --> 00:37:57,208
Has he told you anything yet?

991
00:37:57,242 --> 00:38:00,045
No. But you can
question him if you like.

992
00:38:00,078 --> 00:38:01,580
He's supposed to speak
a little English.

993
00:38:01,613 --> 00:38:02,848
At least, that's
what the résumé said.

994
00:38:02,882 --> 00:38:06,218
- Miguel? Miguel?
- His name is Moisha.

995
00:38:06,252 --> 00:38:07,753
You see, señorita?

996
00:38:07,787 --> 00:38:08,820
You better come with me.

997
00:38:08,853 --> 00:38:10,588
I will find a place for
you to spend the night.

998
00:38:10,622 --> 00:38:11,557
I know.

999
00:38:11,590 --> 00:38:12,858
Thanks, but I'm
going to stay here.

1000
00:38:12,892 --> 00:38:14,893
All night?

1001
00:38:14,927 --> 00:38:16,828
As long as necessary.

1002
00:38:16,862 --> 00:38:19,065
Then my wife better
get you something to eat.

1003
00:38:19,098 --> 00:38:20,632
Thank you.

1004
00:38:22,200 --> 00:38:25,738
Okay Miguel, move
over. I'm hopping in.

1005
00:38:25,772 --> 00:38:28,840
He said dark, and then he
said good, and then he died.

1006
00:38:28,873 --> 00:38:32,912
(gasping)

1007
00:38:32,945 --> 00:38:36,782
Swim, run, huge.
Dean, no, stop.

1008
00:38:36,815 --> 00:38:38,517
What is it Miguel?

1009
00:38:38,550 --> 00:38:43,188
(grunting)
First word, sounds like...

1010
00:38:43,222 --> 00:38:44,557
Hey, he sounds like Eeyore.

1011
00:38:44,590 --> 00:38:45,758
Seargant!

1012
00:38:45,792 --> 00:38:48,360
Could you shut him
up, I'm trying to sleep?

1013
00:38:49,595 --> 00:38:51,430
Get in here,
this is hysterical.

1014
00:38:51,463 --> 00:38:54,500
(grunting continues)

1015
00:38:54,533 --> 00:38:57,837
What now? Jeez. Can't
leave you two alone?

1016
00:39:02,073 --> 00:39:04,777
Oil can? Oil can?

1017
00:39:10,215 --> 00:39:11,817
He's crying for his mother.

1018
00:39:11,850 --> 00:39:13,852
Her name was Uuugh.

1019
00:39:13,885 --> 00:39:18,123
I do not understand what
he's trying to say señorita.

1020
00:39:18,157 --> 00:39:20,658
Call me if he speaks again.

1021
00:39:20,692 --> 00:39:22,762
(screams loudly)
He spoke again!

1022
00:39:22,795 --> 00:39:25,663
Ogro! Ogro!

1023
00:39:25,697 --> 00:39:28,267
Oh, I get it. He's
channeling Frances Farmer.

1024
00:39:28,300 --> 00:39:29,668
Miguel.

1025
00:39:29,702 --> 00:39:31,770
Shouldn't eat chili
peppers, they burned his gut.

1026
00:39:31,803 --> 00:39:34,840
Miguel. Miguel.

1027
00:39:34,873 --> 00:39:38,643
I had Jell-o today.

1028
00:39:38,677 --> 00:39:39,878
All right, you got the part.

1029
00:39:39,912 --> 00:39:41,347
What did he mean?

1030
00:39:41,380 --> 00:39:42,748
It's hard to say.

1031
00:39:42,782 --> 00:39:43,782
It's a guy thing.

1032
00:39:43,815 --> 00:39:45,818
Ogro, what is that?

1033
00:39:45,852 --> 00:39:51,523
It's a big fellow, like an
ogre in a story, a monster.

1034
00:39:51,557 --> 00:39:52,658
A giant.

1035
00:39:52,692 --> 00:39:54,827
He must have been dreaming.

1036
00:39:54,860 --> 00:39:57,295
Was it a giant, Miguel?

1037
00:39:57,328 --> 00:39:59,398
- Was it a giant man?
- Did it make you mad?

1038
00:39:59,432 --> 00:40:01,833
- Did it make you mean mad?
- Was it, Miguel?

1039
00:40:01,867 --> 00:40:04,537
(classical music)

1040
00:40:04,570 --> 00:40:06,805
Might have better
luck when he's awake.

1041
00:40:06,838 --> 00:40:11,810
That's our boy, we'll
be back after these words.

1042
00:40:11,843 --> 00:40:14,447
I remember this scene.

1043
00:40:14,480 --> 00:40:15,713
Hey, it's the guy
that was standing

1044
00:40:15,747 --> 00:40:17,115
next to Oswald when he got shot.

1045
00:40:17,148 --> 00:40:18,617
I just wanted to let you
know, I got your phone call.

1046
00:40:18,650 --> 00:40:20,152
This is Dr. Carmichael.

1047
00:40:20,185 --> 00:40:21,487
He's head of our
radiation excursion.

1048
00:40:21,520 --> 00:40:22,755
Hello, Dr. Carmichael.

1049
00:40:22,788 --> 00:40:24,157
He's very much interested
in your brother's case.

1050
00:40:24,190 --> 00:40:25,858
I only hope I can do
something for him, Ms. Manning.

1051
00:40:25,892 --> 00:40:27,392
Thank you, Doctor.

1052
00:40:27,425 --> 00:40:29,093
Doctor Carmichael,
Major Baird,

1053
00:40:29,128 --> 00:40:31,663
this is Sergeant Mario of
the Mexican State Police.

1054
00:40:31,697 --> 00:40:32,765
Hi.

1055
00:40:32,798 --> 00:40:34,600
Where was it the boy saw
the Colossal Man, the giant?

1056
00:40:34,633 --> 00:40:37,703
El Ogro? He has not
been able to tell us.

1057
00:40:37,737 --> 00:40:38,670
The sergeant
can show you where

1058
00:40:38,703 --> 00:40:40,272
the truck disappeared though.

1059
00:40:40,305 --> 00:40:42,842
Si, señor. Won't you
get in my automobile?

1060
00:40:42,875 --> 00:40:43,742
Fine.

1061
00:40:43,775 --> 00:40:45,978
Fine, but I don't
want to ride in back,

1062
00:40:46,012 --> 00:40:48,813
I've always got to ride in back.

1063
00:40:51,617 --> 00:40:54,953
[all sing] 100 years of
solitude, 100 years of solitude,

1064
00:40:54,987 --> 00:41:00,592
take one down, pass it
around, 99 years of solitude.

1065
00:41:00,625 --> 00:41:02,928
These tracks were made
by the truck he was driving.

1066
00:41:02,962 --> 00:41:06,965
The truck apparently
skidded into the water,

1067
00:41:06,998 --> 00:41:08,600
and never drove out.

1068
00:41:08,633 --> 00:41:10,368
Thank you, Mr. Obvious.

1069
00:41:10,402 --> 00:41:11,403
What do you think, Mark?

1070
00:41:11,437 --> 00:41:12,572
What did happen to Oswald?

1071
00:41:12,605 --> 00:41:13,905
Well, it seems like
pretty meager evidence

1072
00:41:13,938 --> 00:41:15,875
to conclude because
a truck disappeared,

1073
00:41:15,908 --> 00:41:17,442
that the Colossal
Man is still alive.

1074
00:41:17,475 --> 00:41:18,743
Oh, my god. Her arms!
Oh, it's her sweater.

1075
00:41:18,777 --> 00:41:19,712
But the boy?

1076
00:41:19,745 --> 00:41:21,747
I'm sure there's some
logical explanation.

1077
00:41:21,780 --> 00:41:27,085
Señores, señores,
come quickly!

1078
00:41:27,118 --> 00:41:30,088
Look at these cute tadpoles.

1079
00:41:31,390 --> 00:41:33,692
That is a very
big foot print.

1080
00:41:33,725 --> 00:41:36,528
Major Baird, is that
enough logic for you?

1081
00:41:36,562 --> 00:41:38,797
The foot that made
that print is about

1082
00:41:38,830 --> 00:41:41,733
30 times the size
of a normal man.

1083
00:41:41,767 --> 00:41:44,135
That would make him
about 60 feet tall.

1084
00:41:44,168 --> 00:41:45,670
Glenn was 60 feet tall.

1085
00:41:45,703 --> 00:41:46,838
Think there's a connection?

1086
00:41:46,872 --> 00:41:48,073
Nah.

1087
00:41:48,107 --> 00:41:50,008
He must have gone that
way, to the mountains.

1088
00:41:50,042 --> 00:41:53,045
To the prairie, to the
oceans white with foam.

1089
00:41:53,078 --> 00:41:56,848
(ominous classical music)

1090
00:41:56,882 --> 00:41:58,583
He always summer's
in the mountains.

1091
00:41:58,617 --> 00:42:01,553
He stays at the beautiful
Mount Airy Lodge.

1092
00:42:02,888 --> 00:42:04,757
You think he could be
up in those mountains?

1093
00:42:04,790 --> 00:42:07,560
Yes, it could be. There
are no people up there.

1094
00:42:07,593 --> 00:42:09,395
Well, let's drive slowly.

1095
00:42:09,428 --> 00:42:11,597
Maybe we could find
some more foot prints.

1096
00:42:11,630 --> 00:42:13,565
Hey, think there's some
place to eat around here?

1097
00:42:13,598 --> 00:42:15,567
I could really go for a
Jack in the Box right now.

1098
00:42:15,600 --> 00:42:16,768
Yeah, that'd be good.

1099
00:42:16,802 --> 00:42:19,305
We're gonna get a
four-door next time.

1100
00:42:19,338 --> 00:42:21,607
Get one of those
breakfast muffins.

1101
00:42:21,640 --> 00:42:23,475
- Just double clutch.
- Really?

1102
00:42:23,508 --> 00:42:25,077
Just get it out of there.

1103
00:42:25,110 --> 00:42:27,580
Just grind it and find it.

1104
00:42:27,613 --> 00:42:29,415
That's right, spare
us nothing Burt.

1105
00:42:29,448 --> 00:42:30,682
Let's get out of here.

1106
00:42:30,715 --> 00:42:34,620
Three Jacks and a Jill
and three Jills in a Jeep.

1107
00:42:34,653 --> 00:42:36,988
On American Classics.

1108
00:42:39,292 --> 00:42:44,128
(new wave music)

1109
00:42:45,163 --> 00:42:47,933
Who's that guy
with the big head?

1110
00:42:47,967 --> 00:42:49,368
Big head.

1111
00:42:49,402 --> 00:42:50,668
Big head.

1112
00:42:50,702 --> 00:42:53,772
Who's that guy with
the big, big head?

1113
00:42:53,805 --> 00:42:55,540
- Big head.
- Big head.

1114
00:42:55,573 --> 00:42:57,943
Big head.
[all] Big head! Yeah! Whoo!

1115
00:42:57,977 --> 00:43:00,612
OK, that was pretty good,
but let's try it again,

1116
00:43:00,645 --> 00:43:01,913
and let's try to
stay in the same key

1117
00:43:01,947 --> 00:43:03,682
and begin at the same time, OK?

1118
00:43:03,715 --> 00:43:05,417
All right, yeah.

1119
00:43:05,450 --> 00:43:06,618
A one, a two, a one two--

1120
00:43:06,652 --> 00:43:08,587
(loud banging)

1121
00:43:08,620 --> 00:43:09,422
Oh, let's try it again.

1122
00:43:09,455 --> 00:43:11,223
Ok.

1123
00:43:11,257 --> 00:43:12,858
A one, a two, a one two--

1124
00:43:12,892 --> 00:43:14,192
(loud banging)

1125
00:43:14,225 --> 00:43:15,460
Crow, would you cut that out?

1126
00:43:15,493 --> 00:43:16,628
I'm not doin' anything.

1127
00:43:16,662 --> 00:43:18,363
(loud banging)

1128
00:43:18,397 --> 00:43:19,532
Joel, it's not
us, it sounds like

1129
00:43:19,565 --> 00:43:20,932
it's coming from
outside the ship.

1130
00:43:20,967 --> 00:43:22,567
Hello?

1131
00:43:22,600 --> 00:43:23,802
- Anybody home?
- Oh, my goodness.

1132
00:43:23,835 --> 00:43:25,637
Hey Cambot, give me
rocket number nine, quick.

1133
00:43:25,670 --> 00:43:27,840
I want to see the
exterior of the ship.

1134
00:43:27,873 --> 00:43:31,810
Oh, oh there you
are. I heard music.

1135
00:43:31,843 --> 00:43:34,947
You weren't talking
about me, were you?

1136
00:43:34,980 --> 00:43:37,315
Ditch the head.

1137
00:43:37,348 --> 00:43:39,150
Hi, Glenn, good
to see you again.

1138
00:43:39,183 --> 00:43:40,685
How you been, buddy?

1139
00:43:40,718 --> 00:43:41,853
Yeah, did you ever find out

1140
00:43:41,887 --> 00:43:44,423
about that whole
share-rib thing?

1141
00:43:44,457 --> 00:43:45,857
Hey, Crow come
on take it easy.

1142
00:43:45,890 --> 00:43:49,495
Mr. Manning doesn't have time
for your little shenanigans.

1143
00:43:49,528 --> 00:43:50,795
Hey, you know what?

1144
00:43:50,828 --> 00:43:54,032
We were just watching an
experiment about you, Glenn.

1145
00:43:54,065 --> 00:43:58,203
(chewing) I'm sorry.

1146
00:43:59,705 --> 00:44:02,742
I walked all the way over from
the other side of the planet.

1147
00:44:02,775 --> 00:44:06,245
You know, I've been
picking up transmissions

1148
00:44:06,278 --> 00:44:09,247
of your experiments since
I saw you last time,

1149
00:44:09,280 --> 00:44:11,650
I had a whole head full
of goofy dental work.

1150
00:44:11,683 --> 00:44:12,785
You know, I'm a
little torqued off

1151
00:44:12,818 --> 00:44:15,653
about this whole
cheesy sequel thing.

1152
00:44:15,687 --> 00:44:18,357
Yeah, we're all wondering
why you weren't in this one.

1153
00:44:18,390 --> 00:44:20,658
That's what I'm
goin' on about.

1154
00:44:20,692 --> 00:44:22,260
You know, I create this role,

1155
00:44:22,293 --> 00:44:23,695
do this work in the first film.

1156
00:44:23,728 --> 00:44:25,630
Everybody loved my work.

1157
00:44:25,663 --> 00:44:27,065
Excuse me.

1158
00:44:27,098 --> 00:44:29,133
And then, you know
everything's going great.

1159
00:44:29,167 --> 00:44:30,802
Burt and I are good
friends, and then

1160
00:44:30,835 --> 00:44:35,573
the studio's gonna do a sequel.

1161
00:44:35,607 --> 00:44:38,243
I figure it's a done
deal basically, right?

1162
00:44:38,277 --> 00:44:40,378
I'm the perfect man for the job.

1163
00:44:40,412 --> 00:44:42,613
But then, the phone
stops ringing.

1164
00:44:42,647 --> 00:44:43,748
People I thought were my friends

1165
00:44:43,782 --> 00:44:46,118
don't even look me
in the eye anymore.

1166
00:44:46,152 --> 00:44:47,820
(mumbles)

1167
00:44:47,853 --> 00:44:50,088
They pulled a Roger Moore
on me is what they did.

1168
00:44:50,122 --> 00:44:54,660
I thought I was gonna be in
the film, and then nothing.

1169
00:44:54,693 --> 00:44:58,030
I'm sorry. Showbusiness.
I could go on for hours.

1170
00:44:58,063 --> 00:45:00,565
But I didn't come here
to bore, I'm sorry.

1171
00:45:00,598 --> 00:45:03,035
Well, why did you
come here big guy?

1172
00:45:03,068 --> 00:45:07,572
Oh, it was because
I had something...

1173
00:45:07,605 --> 00:45:11,310
I forgot it. I don't
have any pockets.

1174
00:45:11,343 --> 00:45:12,710
Kind of hard to--

1175
00:45:12,745 --> 00:45:15,813
Whoa, whoa. I'm gettin' kind
of a weird signal there.

1176
00:45:15,847 --> 00:45:18,483
It's probably comin'
off your satellite dish.

1177
00:45:18,517 --> 00:45:20,885
It's Golden Girls. Yeah.

1178
00:45:20,918 --> 00:45:23,922
I'm gonna hang around outside
here if you don't mind,

1179
00:45:23,955 --> 00:45:27,725
I kinda have a thing
for Bea Arthur.

1180
00:45:27,760 --> 00:45:29,327
I'll just hang and wait--

1181
00:45:29,360 --> 00:45:32,297
(grunts loudly)

1182
00:45:32,330 --> 00:45:34,165
[all] Whoa! Ah!
(buzzers)

1183
00:45:34,198 --> 00:45:36,768
We got a movie sign. Ah!

1184
00:45:36,802 --> 00:45:41,807
(doors open hydraulically)

1185
00:45:46,812 --> 00:45:50,882
[all singing] 52 years of
solitude, 52 years of solitude,

1186
00:45:50,915 --> 00:45:53,818
Ha-ha, I like that.

1187
00:45:54,820 --> 00:45:55,987
Excuse me, I'll
just be a minute.

1188
00:45:56,020 --> 00:45:58,623
I'm cramping up here. Excuse me.

1189
00:45:58,657 --> 00:46:00,425
Must've been that changa.

1190
00:46:00,458 --> 00:46:01,727
What do you see, Louis?

1191
00:46:01,760 --> 00:46:02,627
There are no more footprints,

1192
00:46:02,660 --> 00:46:03,895
the ground is too hard here.

1193
00:46:03,928 --> 00:46:05,397
I see something over there.

1194
00:46:05,430 --> 00:46:09,200
Look! It's a big turd
with track marks in it.

1195
00:46:10,735 --> 00:46:12,805
Hey, there's a Hard
Rock Cafe over there.

1196
00:46:12,838 --> 00:46:14,940
They're everywhere now.

1197
00:46:14,973 --> 00:46:18,043
(ominous classical music)

1198
00:46:21,413 --> 00:46:24,248
Oh no, Glenn passed
a truck on the highway.

1199
00:46:24,282 --> 00:46:26,852
I mean he passed a
truck on the highway.

1200
00:46:26,885 --> 00:46:29,622
This must be part of
Swanson's missing truck.

1201
00:46:29,655 --> 00:46:30,922
No, this another
truck señorita,

1202
00:46:30,955 --> 00:46:34,660
Swanson's truck is
dark green (mumbles).

1203
00:46:34,693 --> 00:46:35,827
Yeah, but it's
bound to change color

1204
00:46:35,860 --> 00:46:38,063
after it's been
through his system.

1205
00:46:38,097 --> 00:46:40,065
Tom, will you stop with
the crap jokes please?

1206
00:46:40,098 --> 00:46:42,333
Hey, Wile E. Coyote
must be up there,

1207
00:46:42,367 --> 00:46:44,268
to change to subject.

1208
00:46:44,302 --> 00:46:45,803
Hey, want a Rolling Rock?

1209
00:46:45,837 --> 00:46:48,973
(laughs)
Kidding.

1210
00:46:49,007 --> 00:46:51,177
Nothing but a rock slide.

1211
00:46:51,210 --> 00:46:54,647
It's getting late.
We better go home.

1212
00:46:54,680 --> 00:46:56,715
It's not good to
be here after dark.

1213
00:46:56,748 --> 00:47:00,452
That's right, you
cannot trust me after dark.

1214
00:47:00,485 --> 00:47:02,855
I was gettin' tired anyway.

1215
00:47:02,888 --> 00:47:04,890
Man, it's been a big day huh?

1216
00:47:04,923 --> 00:47:06,392
What did you think
of those big rocks?

1217
00:47:06,425 --> 00:47:08,527
What were they all about anyway?

1218
00:47:08,560 --> 00:47:10,462
Oh let's knock off.

1219
00:47:10,495 --> 00:47:13,965
And the Grinch peered down
with his little dog, Max.

1220
00:47:13,998 --> 00:47:18,303
(slurping and burping)

1221
00:47:18,337 --> 00:47:18,870
Pardon me.

1222
00:47:18,903 --> 00:47:20,072
New York City.

1223
00:47:20,105 --> 00:47:21,038
Just pretend to
eat it and fold it

1224
00:47:21,072 --> 00:47:23,542
in your napkin and say, "Mmm".

1225
00:47:23,575 --> 00:47:24,710
Oh, no thanks.

1226
00:47:24,743 --> 00:47:25,610
Oh, no, no, no.
I'm going to get

1227
00:47:25,643 --> 00:47:29,347
the Aztec two-step as it is.

1228
00:47:29,380 --> 00:47:30,548
Sorry to be so long.

1229
00:47:30,582 --> 00:47:31,650
Bragger.

1230
00:47:31,683 --> 00:47:32,750
But I just talked
to the Mexican

1231
00:47:32,783 --> 00:47:33,918
Military Authorities
on the phone.

1232
00:47:33,952 --> 00:47:35,153
Couldn't understand a word.

1233
00:47:35,187 --> 00:47:36,788
They have troops and
artillery standing by.

1234
00:47:36,822 --> 00:47:39,023
All we have to do is
pinpoint the Colossal Man,

1235
00:47:39,057 --> 00:47:40,792
and they'll move in on him.

1236
00:47:40,825 --> 00:47:43,128
He might come willingly
if we reasoned with him.

1237
00:47:43,162 --> 00:47:44,162
Nuh-uh.

1238
00:47:44,195 --> 00:47:45,697
How do you reason
with a 60 foot giant?

1239
00:47:45,730 --> 00:47:46,832
It's possible he'd
listen to someone

1240
00:47:46,865 --> 00:47:48,633
he knows well,
like Miss Manning.

1241
00:47:48,667 --> 00:47:50,068
Ah, eat your
fajita and shut up.

1242
00:47:50,102 --> 00:47:52,003
No, I don't want
anybody getting hurt.

1243
00:47:52,037 --> 00:47:53,672
Would someone please
pass the sour cream?

1244
00:47:53,705 --> 00:47:55,773
Please let me try at least.

1245
00:47:55,807 --> 00:47:56,808
No, I'm sorry.

1246
00:47:56,842 --> 00:47:57,808
Be reasonable,
if you use force

1247
00:47:57,842 --> 00:47:59,978
on him, somebody will get hurt.

1248
00:48:00,012 --> 00:48:01,713
Nope, we're closed. Sorry.

1249
00:48:01,747 --> 00:48:03,515
Very well then.
I'll go alone.

1250
00:48:03,548 --> 00:48:04,482
And I'll force myself on him.

1251
00:48:04,515 --> 00:48:06,518
Wait. Miss Manning!

1252
00:48:07,752 --> 00:48:10,355
Miss Manning, I
didn't ask for this job,

1253
00:48:10,388 --> 00:48:11,590
I was assigned to it.

1254
00:48:11,623 --> 00:48:12,690
And if I'm going to get it done,

1255
00:48:12,723 --> 00:48:14,392
I'll have to handle
it my own way.

1256
00:48:14,425 --> 00:48:15,393
Well it seems to me your way

1257
00:48:15,427 --> 00:48:17,228
is going to hurt him
more than help him.

1258
00:48:17,262 --> 00:48:19,865
I have to think of the
safety of others as well as him.

1259
00:48:19,898 --> 00:48:20,998
That goes for you too.

1260
00:48:21,032 --> 00:48:22,433
It's my way or the highway.

1261
00:48:22,467 --> 00:48:24,502
I'm to forget he's my brother
and do nothing, I suppose?

1262
00:48:24,535 --> 00:48:26,572
It's natural for you
to be concerned about him.

1263
00:48:26,605 --> 00:48:27,438
Natural.

1264
00:48:27,472 --> 00:48:28,740
Can't you let it go at that?

1265
00:48:28,773 --> 00:48:30,342
You don't even know if
he'll recognize you or not.

1266
00:48:30,375 --> 00:48:31,477
He won't even
be able to see you.

1267
00:48:31,510 --> 00:48:32,810
Let me find out.

1268
00:48:32,843 --> 00:48:34,780
We'll see about it tomorrow
when the soldiers arrive.

1269
00:48:34,813 --> 00:48:38,483
Today please, before
they get here. Please.

1270
00:48:38,517 --> 00:48:39,550
What makes you so sure
you're going to find him?

1271
00:48:39,583 --> 00:48:40,952
He's 60 flippin' feet tall!

1272
00:48:40,985 --> 00:48:43,388
There's a whole
mountain he has to hide in.

1273
00:48:43,422 --> 00:48:47,758
We have something to
go on. Those footprints.

1274
00:48:47,792 --> 00:48:49,527
All right. Get in my car.

1275
00:48:49,560 --> 00:48:53,532
I guess the great
sister of Glenn knows best.

1276
00:48:53,565 --> 00:48:55,667
I'll drive,
scoot over. Scoot.

1277
00:48:55,700 --> 00:48:57,935
(ominous classical music)

1278
00:48:57,970 --> 00:48:59,003
You sure this is
where William Holden

1279
00:48:59,037 --> 00:49:01,340
did that scene in Picnic?

1280
00:49:01,373 --> 00:49:05,210
Hey, let's drop this whole
Glenn thing, I love you.

1281
00:49:07,278 --> 00:49:11,082
Well, they're in the valley
of the Jolly Green Giant.

1282
00:49:12,985 --> 00:49:15,853
Maybe they should get
Crazy Horse up on that...

1283
00:49:15,887 --> 00:49:17,888
Not enough time.

1284
00:49:17,922 --> 00:49:20,825
Why are they playing
The Last Emperor music?!

1285
00:49:20,858 --> 00:49:22,760
Beats me.

1286
00:49:22,793 --> 00:49:25,297
Classic.

1287
00:49:25,330 --> 00:49:28,633
Well look, it's the
Fred Sanford memorial.

1288
00:49:29,935 --> 00:49:31,068
A litter box.

1289
00:49:31,102 --> 00:49:32,537
"This'll be our home honey.

1290
00:49:32,570 --> 00:49:36,642
"We'll put the living room over
here, just like we dreamed."

1291
00:49:36,675 --> 00:49:39,812
Looks more like a Wisconsin
farmer's frightening lawn.

1292
00:49:39,845 --> 00:49:41,680
(laughs)

1293
00:49:45,417 --> 00:49:48,087
Similac. What's
he need that for?

1294
00:49:48,120 --> 00:49:50,822
He's been raiding these
trucks for food to live on.

1295
00:49:50,855 --> 00:49:52,490
Jerk, didn't even pay.

1296
00:49:55,260 --> 00:49:56,595
He's uses this
as a tooth pick!

1297
00:49:56,628 --> 00:49:59,563
That's for the gang that
couldn't shoot straight.

1298
00:49:59,597 --> 00:50:01,933
(laughs) A little prop humor.

1299
00:50:01,967 --> 00:50:04,235
Major. Look at this.

1300
00:50:08,773 --> 00:50:10,708
Nope. That's not
Glenn's, the whole

1301
00:50:10,742 --> 00:50:13,445
whirl is completely different.

1302
00:50:13,478 --> 00:50:14,847
This was Swanson's truck.

1303
00:50:14,880 --> 00:50:17,782
Gosh, you're good
my little detective.

1304
00:50:17,815 --> 00:50:19,985
(rumbling)

1305
00:50:20,018 --> 00:50:23,288
- What's that?
- I don't know.

1306
00:50:23,322 --> 00:50:24,957
[all scream]

1307
00:50:24,990 --> 00:50:27,458
Hey, that's what the
Mexican kid was sayin'.

1308
00:50:27,492 --> 00:50:30,295
(dark classical music)

1309
00:50:31,763 --> 00:50:33,532
You've looked better, Glenn.

1310
00:50:33,565 --> 00:50:35,867
"Oh, hi sis."

1311
00:50:35,900 --> 00:50:38,137
Get back, Joyce!

1312
00:50:39,972 --> 00:50:44,408
"Where was I? Now
where's my surprise toy?

1313
00:50:45,777 --> 00:50:47,978
"Ah, phooey.

1314
00:50:48,880 --> 00:50:50,782
"Ah, nothin.

1315
00:50:50,815 --> 00:50:55,087
"This is the last
time I high-jack and
organic food truck."

1316
00:50:56,888 --> 00:50:59,023
Sounds like Tim Allen.

1317
00:50:59,057 --> 00:51:02,127
(robots humming)

1318
00:51:05,263 --> 00:51:07,332
Oh no, I couldn't. I
couldn't eat another bite.

1319
00:51:07,365 --> 00:51:09,000
No, not bread.

1320
00:51:15,840 --> 00:51:17,042
I like to pick
out the raisins.

1321
00:51:17,075 --> 00:51:21,312
They are baking it the
way you want it, señor.

1322
00:51:21,345 --> 00:51:23,715
Put some tarragon
and mayo on it.

1323
00:51:23,748 --> 00:51:24,883
When will the rest be done?

1324
00:51:24,917 --> 00:51:26,317
They say it
would be ready soon.

1325
00:51:26,350 --> 00:51:28,287
They already loading the truck.

1326
00:51:28,320 --> 00:51:29,487
Good.

1327
00:51:29,520 --> 00:51:31,690
Want a cold cut with that?
A slice of cheese maybe?

1328
00:51:31,723 --> 00:51:33,292
Pickle?

1329
00:51:35,027 --> 00:51:36,595
Try it.

1330
00:51:36,628 --> 00:51:41,098
It really is delightful
bread. Mmm, flavor.

1331
00:51:41,132 --> 00:51:43,000
Can you taste anything?

1332
00:51:43,033 --> 00:51:45,903
Tastes like
bread. What's in it?

1333
00:51:45,937 --> 00:51:46,938
Chloral hydrate.

1334
00:51:46,972 --> 00:51:48,407
[all] (spitting with disgust)

1335
00:51:48,440 --> 00:51:51,610
It makes you go to
sleep for eight hours.

1336
00:51:52,810 --> 00:51:54,278
I'll be at the bakery.

1337
00:51:54,312 --> 00:51:57,315
With the butcher and
the candlestick maker.

1338
00:51:59,550 --> 00:52:02,253
We have to capture him and
this seems to be the best way.

1339
00:52:02,287 --> 00:52:03,622
Fill him up on bread?

1340
00:52:03,655 --> 00:52:05,357
Dr. Carmichael says it
can't harm him in the least.

1341
00:52:05,390 --> 00:52:08,627
How do you
handle a hungry man?

1342
00:52:08,660 --> 00:52:10,762
Isn't there any
other way to handle it?

1343
00:52:10,795 --> 00:52:11,930
No, we thought
about Saltines--

1344
00:52:11,963 --> 00:52:14,065
Let the Mexican
Authorities deal with him.

1345
00:52:14,098 --> 00:52:15,500
We have no other alternatives.

1346
00:52:15,533 --> 00:52:16,835
Nobody's taken the
time to think of any,

1347
00:52:16,868 --> 00:52:18,837
as far as I can see.

1348
00:52:18,870 --> 00:52:20,505
There isn't time to take.

1349
00:52:20,538 --> 00:52:22,440
If we don't get him before
he decides to move to another

1350
00:52:22,473 --> 00:52:25,310
mountain, we may lose
contact with him for weeks.

1351
00:52:25,343 --> 00:52:28,280
Yeah, Lord knows
he never writes.

1352
00:52:29,680 --> 00:52:31,650
I suppose you're right.

1353
00:52:31,683 --> 00:52:32,650
Yup.

1354
00:52:32,683 --> 00:52:37,255
His face. I can't
forget how horrible it was.

1355
00:52:37,288 --> 00:52:38,690
Where are you from, Joyce?

1356
00:52:38,723 --> 00:52:40,158
My home is in San Francisco.

1357
00:52:40,192 --> 00:52:41,593
But I live in Brittania.

1358
00:52:41,627 --> 00:52:42,793
Don't you think you'd
be better off back there?

1359
00:52:42,827 --> 00:52:43,962
Do you know what
it would be like

1360
00:52:43,995 --> 00:52:47,232
just sitting around
waiting for news?

1361
00:52:47,265 --> 00:52:48,700
You mind if I
suggest a remedy?

1362
00:52:48,733 --> 00:52:49,800
Try some bread.

1363
00:52:49,833 --> 00:52:50,935
It's an old one
but it usually works.

1364
00:52:50,968 --> 00:52:54,172
Find something else
to occupy your mind.

1365
00:52:54,205 --> 00:52:55,007
Sports, or somethin'.

1366
00:52:55,040 --> 00:52:56,273
Do you have a job?

1367
00:52:56,307 --> 00:52:58,543
I write copy for
an advertising agency.

1368
00:52:58,577 --> 00:53:00,778
Well, that should help
you forget your troubles.

1369
00:53:00,812 --> 00:53:02,247
I can imagine
myself going back

1370
00:53:02,280 --> 00:53:05,117
to writing all those
tired, old adjectives.

1371
00:53:05,150 --> 00:53:07,618
Tremendous, gigantic, colossal.

1372
00:53:07,652 --> 00:53:09,853
You know what'd they mean
to me now, don't you?

1373
00:53:09,888 --> 00:53:14,558
Glenn. A colossal freak major.

1374
00:53:14,592 --> 00:53:15,593
And he's my brother.

1375
00:53:15,627 --> 00:53:16,995
Look lady, we all
have our cross to bear.

1376
00:53:17,028 --> 00:53:20,132
We're all set major.

1377
00:53:22,600 --> 00:53:24,668
Time to make donuts.

1378
00:53:26,437 --> 00:53:29,673
(depressing classical music)

1379
00:53:29,707 --> 00:53:34,612
He ain't heavy. He's
my brother (crying).

1380
00:53:34,645 --> 00:53:36,280
Yep, that's
where we'll probably

1381
00:53:36,313 --> 00:53:38,015
put the butter over there.

1382
00:53:38,048 --> 00:53:39,918
Somethin' like that.

1383
00:53:42,853 --> 00:53:45,857
Secure the area where
you tracked Swanson's truck.

1384
00:53:45,890 --> 00:53:48,260
You ain't the boss of me.

1385
00:53:48,293 --> 00:53:49,893
Think he'll eat this stuff?

1386
00:53:49,928 --> 00:53:51,630
All depends on his appetite.

1387
00:53:51,663 --> 00:53:52,763
There hasn't
been a truck through

1388
00:53:52,797 --> 00:53:54,132
this road for two days now.

1389
00:53:54,165 --> 00:53:55,467
He should be hungry.

1390
00:53:55,500 --> 00:53:57,602
Let me drive. I
know this road best.

1391
00:53:57,635 --> 00:54:00,372
All right. There's
still no sign of him.

1392
00:54:00,405 --> 00:54:03,808
If we don't make contact,
we'll make the trip again.

1393
00:54:03,842 --> 00:54:05,310
A bread truck named Desire.

1394
00:54:05,343 --> 00:54:08,480
Tonight on Tennessee
Ernie Williams Theater.

1395
00:54:11,650 --> 00:54:14,452
Hey, let go of me!
What are you doing señor?

1396
00:54:14,485 --> 00:54:17,722
Hey, I've got a job to do!

1397
00:54:17,755 --> 00:54:22,127
Yoo-hoo, just drivin'
a bread truck.

1398
00:54:22,160 --> 00:54:26,363
Fresh bread, mouthwatering
bread. Sliced thin.

1399
00:54:26,397 --> 00:54:29,233
Enjoy our bread
and keep the truck.

1400
00:54:29,267 --> 00:54:30,468
(sings) Somethin'
new is comin' to town,

1401
00:54:30,502 --> 00:54:32,337
and Jose the cop
is bringin' around.

1402
00:54:32,370 --> 00:54:35,640
(mysterious classical music)

1403
00:54:37,642 --> 00:54:38,610
Whoa.

1404
00:54:38,643 --> 00:54:39,843
Because we're
looking for a giant

1405
00:54:39,877 --> 00:54:42,580
that doesn't follow that
we don't value our lives.

1406
00:54:42,613 --> 00:54:45,583
Besides, we want
him to catch us.

1407
00:54:45,617 --> 00:54:48,252
At this speed,
he'll never make it.

1408
00:54:48,285 --> 00:54:50,455
I don't drive this way to
make you nervous, señores.

1409
00:54:50,488 --> 00:54:53,190
I'm driving this way 'cause
I ate some of that bread.

1410
00:54:53,223 --> 00:54:55,293
The further away (mumbles).

1411
00:54:55,327 --> 00:54:58,697
Besides, giants run very
fast. They have long legs.

1412
00:54:58,730 --> 00:55:02,833
Suddenly, you're the
expert on giants, huh?

1413
00:55:04,768 --> 00:55:07,172
The Bonneville Salt Flats,
where the world bread truck

1414
00:55:07,205 --> 00:55:11,108
land-speed record
trials are held.

1415
00:55:11,142 --> 00:55:12,710
Truckin' like
the Doo-Dah man.

1416
00:55:12,743 --> 00:55:14,778
He's right behind us.
You better step on it.

1417
00:55:14,812 --> 00:55:15,847
Before he does.

1418
00:55:15,880 --> 00:55:20,052
(suspenseful classical music)

1419
00:55:20,085 --> 00:55:22,520
Giant gentlemen prefer Hanes.

1420
00:55:22,553 --> 00:55:23,355
Nice gams.

1421
00:55:23,388 --> 00:55:25,490
You're getting close.

1422
00:55:25,523 --> 00:55:27,825
When we get to the
clearing, we'll slow down.

1423
00:55:27,858 --> 00:55:29,760
We better jump out.

1424
00:55:29,793 --> 00:55:31,028
If we make it.

1425
00:55:31,062 --> 00:55:32,697
We will.

1426
00:55:32,730 --> 00:55:35,133
Get ready.

1427
00:55:35,167 --> 00:55:37,502
Let's do this
again in 10 years.

1428
00:55:38,437 --> 00:55:42,840
Ready? Ready? Run!

1429
00:55:48,245 --> 00:55:50,915
Looks like an
episode of Cop Rock.

1430
00:55:50,948 --> 00:55:52,050
'Cause the rock and the...

1431
00:55:52,083 --> 00:55:53,183
(groans)

1432
00:55:53,217 --> 00:55:54,718
I know. I'm sorry

1433
00:55:56,353 --> 00:56:00,225
Roar. I smell something good.

1434
00:56:00,258 --> 00:56:01,625
Find a Chevy
truck, pick it up,

1435
00:56:01,658 --> 00:56:04,195
all day long you'll
have good luck.

1436
00:56:04,228 --> 00:56:07,165
Pretty truck.

1437
00:56:07,198 --> 00:56:10,635
(grunts)

1438
00:56:10,668 --> 00:56:14,605
What a pig. I mean he's
big and all but, really.

1439
00:56:14,638 --> 00:56:16,440
I think he likes
it. He's goin' for it.

1440
00:56:16,473 --> 00:56:19,810
He likes the rye. I told you
we shoulda made more rye.

1441
00:56:22,147 --> 00:56:26,050
What's your soup today?

1442
00:56:26,083 --> 00:56:28,720
Oh no! We smell like bread!

1443
00:56:28,753 --> 00:56:30,955
[all] Nooo!

1444
00:56:30,988 --> 00:56:33,625
Waiter, oh waiter?

1445
00:56:33,658 --> 00:56:37,328
Oh no, it backfired.
He's grown in 12 ways.

1446
00:56:37,362 --> 00:56:41,767
(suspensful classical music)

1447
00:56:41,800 --> 00:56:45,370
He ate too fast, it's
gone right to his head.

1448
00:56:45,403 --> 00:56:48,907
He's got a yeast-over.

1449
00:56:48,940 --> 00:56:50,575
KTLA predicts...

1450
00:56:50,608 --> 00:56:52,310
The case of Army
Colonel and Manning,

1451
00:56:52,343 --> 00:56:55,247
The Colossal Man who
went berserk in Las
Vegas not long ago,

1452
00:56:55,280 --> 00:56:57,415
- is in the news again today.
- Remember that?

1453
00:56:57,448 --> 00:56:58,617
He must have a good agent.

1454
00:56:58,650 --> 00:56:59,850
Military authorities
last night admitted,

1455
00:56:59,883 --> 00:57:01,585
that the earlier
announcement he had been

1456
00:57:01,618 --> 00:57:04,088
accidentally destroyed
was an error.

1457
00:57:04,122 --> 00:57:05,223
Hey look, nobody's perfect.

1458
00:57:05,257 --> 00:57:07,558
He was captured
alive today in Mexico.

1459
00:57:07,592 --> 00:57:08,993
And plans are underway
to fly him back

1460
00:57:09,027 --> 00:57:12,297
across the border in a
troop carrier transport.

1461
00:57:12,330 --> 00:57:14,498
Thing.

1462
00:57:14,532 --> 00:57:15,700
Tonight, the Kennedy Center

1463
00:57:15,733 --> 00:57:17,668
for the Arts presents Pavarotti.

1464
00:57:17,702 --> 00:57:19,737
You may quote me in
saying that the Nation

1465
00:57:19,770 --> 00:57:22,007
and its Representatives
and Congress have nothing

1466
00:57:22,040 --> 00:57:25,577
but gratitude for our Sister
Republic south of the border.

1467
00:57:25,610 --> 00:57:27,412
For their unstinting
cooperation.

1468
00:57:27,445 --> 00:57:28,445
And their bread.

1469
00:57:28,480 --> 00:57:29,813
And we're proud
of Major Baird,

1470
00:57:29,847 --> 00:57:32,650
and the way he handled the
problem of the Colossal Man.

1471
00:57:32,683 --> 00:57:34,585
It was a good job all around.

1472
00:57:34,618 --> 00:57:35,587
That's all for now.

1473
00:57:35,620 --> 00:57:36,687
Well, now that
he's being brought

1474
00:57:36,720 --> 00:57:38,288
back to the United States,

1475
00:57:38,322 --> 00:57:40,825
what does Congress
plan to do about him?

1476
00:57:40,858 --> 00:57:41,960
Do about whom?

1477
00:57:41,993 --> 00:57:42,960
I withdraw the question.

1478
00:57:42,993 --> 00:57:44,328
The giant man.

1479
00:57:44,362 --> 00:57:45,863
Oh, as far as I
know, that matter

1480
00:57:45,897 --> 00:57:48,098
doesn't come under
congressional jurisdiction.

1481
00:57:48,132 --> 00:57:51,502
I was given to understand the
Department of Medical Research

1482
00:57:51,535 --> 00:57:53,938
takes over from this point on.

1483
00:57:53,972 --> 00:57:55,207
Pass me that buck.

1484
00:57:55,240 --> 00:57:56,307
Fred Murtz?

1485
00:57:56,340 --> 00:57:59,143
More like bundts
and honeydew.

1486
00:57:59,177 --> 00:58:02,013
Naturally, we'll do what
we can in a scientific way.

1487
00:58:02,047 --> 00:58:04,048
But it's impossible
for this department

1488
00:58:04,082 --> 00:58:07,352
to assume the responsibility
of his feeding and custody.

1489
00:58:07,385 --> 00:58:08,753
So, we're putting
him up at the Watergate.

1490
00:58:08,787 --> 00:58:10,288
Why don't you take
this question up with

1491
00:58:10,322 --> 00:58:13,758
the Health and
Welfare Department?

1492
00:58:13,792 --> 00:58:15,293
Yes, that's my theme music.

1493
00:58:15,327 --> 00:58:16,460
The man in case was referred

1494
00:58:16,493 --> 00:58:18,597
to us here and
Health and Welfare,

1495
00:58:18,630 --> 00:58:19,897
but we found it laid outside the

1496
00:58:19,930 --> 00:58:22,333
scope of our original
appropriations.

1497
00:58:22,367 --> 00:58:23,768
So we turned him
out in the street.

1498
00:58:23,802 --> 00:58:24,868
I have an idea if he's
turned over to Congress

1499
00:58:24,902 --> 00:58:28,707
for action during
the present session.

1500
00:58:28,740 --> 00:58:31,308
I can't imagine who
told you to call me.

1501
00:58:31,342 --> 00:58:32,243
I told you not
to call me here.

1502
00:58:32,277 --> 00:58:33,410
The problem is it weren't for

1503
00:58:33,443 --> 00:58:34,812
the Legislative
Branch to settle.

1504
00:58:34,845 --> 00:58:37,382
Why don't you call the Pentagon?

1505
00:58:37,415 --> 00:58:41,152
Bert I. Gordon
sticks it to the man.

1506
00:58:41,185 --> 00:58:43,622
Guests of the Pentagon fly
U.S. Military Air Transport.

1507
00:58:43,655 --> 00:58:45,423
Recommended by John Sununu.

1508
00:58:45,457 --> 00:58:47,225
Tell the pilot to
circle the field once more.

1509
00:58:47,258 --> 00:58:48,492
The movie's not quite over.

1510
00:58:48,525 --> 00:58:51,295
U.S. 11034, you're to
circle the field again.

1511
00:58:51,328 --> 00:58:52,763
Roger.

1512
00:58:52,797 --> 00:58:54,332
He has enough fuel left
for five more minutes, Mayor.

1513
00:58:54,365 --> 00:58:55,633
Roger.

1514
00:58:55,667 --> 00:58:56,867
I can't understand you're
not giving your permission

1515
00:58:56,902 --> 00:58:58,135
for him to land under
those circumstances.

1516
00:58:58,168 --> 00:59:00,772
I think I've made our
position pretty clear.

1517
00:59:00,805 --> 00:59:02,773
We have no facilities
for a giant here.

1518
00:59:02,807 --> 00:59:04,242
But the sign said,
"Giants Welcome".

1519
00:59:04,275 --> 00:59:05,377
That plane runs out of gas,

1520
00:59:05,410 --> 00:59:06,710
it's coming down whether
you like it or not.

1521
00:59:06,743 --> 00:59:08,412
It can't stay any
longer, then it's necessary

1522
00:59:08,445 --> 00:59:10,415
to refuel and take off again.

1523
00:59:10,448 --> 00:59:11,582
Take off for where?

1524
00:59:11,615 --> 00:59:13,383
We need time to decide
what to do with him.

1525
00:59:13,417 --> 00:59:16,387
Can't you find us a warehouse
to keep him in temporarily?

1526
00:59:16,420 --> 00:59:19,390
Our warehouses are
all located in the
heart of the city.

1527
00:59:19,423 --> 00:59:20,525
Must be a nice downtown.

1528
00:59:20,558 --> 00:59:21,725
This creature's
presence there

1529
00:59:21,758 --> 00:59:23,293
would constitute too
great a police problem.

1530
00:59:23,327 --> 00:59:25,630
Now that applies to every
other large downtown building

1531
00:59:25,663 --> 00:59:28,065
including the Colosseum.

1532
00:59:28,098 --> 00:59:29,667
Have you considered
the Hollywood Bowl?

1533
00:59:29,700 --> 00:59:30,868
He's not ready for
the Hollywood Bowl.

1534
00:59:30,902 --> 00:59:32,737
But I can't have him
exposed to the weather.

1535
00:59:32,770 --> 00:59:34,005
He'll rust or something.

1536
00:59:34,038 --> 00:59:36,773
Well, I can't make
any further suggestions.

1537
00:59:36,807 --> 00:59:39,977
Well you're a big help.

1538
00:59:39,977 --> 00:59:40,778
Oh.

1539
00:59:41,712 --> 00:59:43,580
That hangar doesn't
seem to be in use.

1540
00:59:43,580 --> 00:59:45,617
Oh yes, my
coats--Oh that hangar.

1541
00:59:45,683 --> 00:59:46,917
That's impossible.

1542
00:59:46,985 --> 00:59:48,318
This airport is
one of the major

1543
00:59:48,385 --> 00:59:50,220
traffic centers of
the Western World.

1544
00:59:50,288 --> 00:59:51,288
That's why we're
so over-staffed.

1545
00:59:51,322 --> 00:59:52,590
Do you realize
what it would mean

1546
00:59:52,623 --> 00:59:54,125
if an uncontrollable monster
should get loose here?

1547
00:59:54,192 --> 00:59:55,527
I give you my word.

1548
00:59:55,593 --> 00:59:56,693
We won't keep him here a minute

1549
00:59:56,693 --> 00:59:59,897
longer than is
absolutely necessary.

1550
00:59:59,897 --> 01:00:00,832
Ok.

1551
01:00:00,898 --> 01:00:01,898
All right.

1552
01:00:01,898 --> 01:00:04,435
Miss Hathaway, tell
them they can land.

1553
01:00:04,502 --> 01:00:06,003
Tell the ship to
come in for a landing.

1554
01:00:06,037 --> 01:00:07,805
Jethro, you can
come in for a landing.

1555
01:00:07,838 --> 01:00:12,143
U.S. 11034, you may
land on runway 1-7-0.

1556
01:00:15,413 --> 01:00:19,717
Hope Glenn took
some Dramamine.

1557
01:00:19,750 --> 01:00:20,618
That big guy was kickin' the

1558
01:00:20,652 --> 01:00:23,220
back of my seat the whole way.

1559
01:00:26,523 --> 01:00:29,727
Hey, look, the fly's open.

1560
01:00:31,628 --> 01:00:33,965
Look, he's levitating.

1561
01:00:34,032 --> 01:00:36,667
Glenn, turn over.

1562
01:00:36,733 --> 01:00:39,237
(grunting)
Sounds like a mini-bike.

1563
01:00:41,738 --> 01:00:43,742
Has anyone seen Glenn?

1564
01:00:43,742 --> 01:00:45,577
Why are you keeping
Glenn here at the airport?

1565
01:00:45,643 --> 01:00:46,777
Lady, do you
ever stop griping?

1566
01:00:46,843 --> 01:00:47,745
I'm holding him
here 'til Washington

1567
01:00:47,778 --> 01:00:49,180
decides what to do with him.

1568
01:00:49,247 --> 01:00:50,548
What to do with him?

1569
01:00:50,582 --> 01:00:52,550
He ought to be in a
hospital getting treatment.

1570
01:00:52,583 --> 01:00:54,452
They don't make
hospitals that big, Joyce.

1571
01:00:54,485 --> 01:00:55,653
Touché.

1572
01:00:55,687 --> 01:00:56,587
Besides, the
Doctors haven't turned

1573
01:00:56,620 --> 01:00:58,155
up anything that can help.

1574
01:00:58,188 --> 01:00:59,923
Why don't they treat
him with sulfhydryl?

1575
01:00:59,957 --> 01:01:01,792
Isn't that what they
said would cure him?

1576
01:01:01,825 --> 01:01:04,528
Well, they found it
stopped his growth but,

1577
01:01:04,562 --> 01:01:06,897
I'm afraid it won't reverse it.

1578
01:01:06,930 --> 01:01:08,198
I see.

1579
01:01:08,232 --> 01:01:10,835
He'll just have to get a
job and try to fit in somehow.

1580
01:01:10,868 --> 01:01:12,370
They can't keep
him here as if

1581
01:01:12,403 --> 01:01:13,905
he was some kind of cattle.

1582
01:01:13,938 --> 01:01:16,507
No, we're keeping him
here 'cause he's a big freak.

1583
01:01:16,540 --> 01:01:18,008
It's just for the time being.

1584
01:01:18,042 --> 01:01:19,710
I'm afraid the world
doesn't think of a

1585
01:01:19,743 --> 01:01:21,945
60 foot man the
way a sister does.

1586
01:01:21,978 --> 01:01:25,750
I read that on a
Hallmark card once.

1587
01:01:25,783 --> 01:01:29,320
Don't touch anything.

1588
01:01:29,353 --> 01:01:32,223
(grunting)

1589
01:01:35,693 --> 01:01:38,897
I'm taking down everything
he says, everything.

1590
01:01:38,930 --> 01:01:41,298
Sergeant Bilko,
what's he doin' here?

1591
01:01:42,800 --> 01:01:45,470
I said I hate futons.

1592
01:01:45,503 --> 01:01:48,605
I suppose it's necessary to
keep him tied down like this.

1593
01:01:48,638 --> 01:01:49,973
Yeah, it's fun too.

1594
01:01:50,007 --> 01:01:51,675
If I could trust him, I
could let him have more freedom.

1595
01:01:51,708 --> 01:01:54,078
As it is, I have
to play it safe.

1596
01:01:54,112 --> 01:01:57,115
This guy's inner child
is bigger than all of us.

1597
01:01:57,148 --> 01:01:58,648
Glenn Manning!

1598
01:01:58,682 --> 01:01:59,483
(screaming)

1599
01:01:59,517 --> 01:02:02,187
Yo, here! Right here.

1600
01:02:02,220 --> 01:02:05,355
As you see, he doesn't
even know his name.

1601
01:02:05,388 --> 01:02:08,692
Colonel Manning!
Colonel Manning!

1602
01:02:08,725 --> 01:02:10,528
You're just doin'
that to annoy him.

1603
01:02:10,562 --> 01:02:11,962
Colonel Manning!

1604
01:02:11,995 --> 01:02:14,965
(grunting)

1605
01:02:14,998 --> 01:02:19,237
What a dope. We are so
much better than he is.

1606
01:02:21,705 --> 01:02:23,707
I gotta scratch my
nose, for Pete's sake.

1607
01:02:23,740 --> 01:02:28,012
(new wave music)

1608
01:02:29,480 --> 01:02:31,448
(grunting)

1609
01:02:31,482 --> 01:02:33,750
Ready? Ah!

1610
01:02:33,785 --> 01:02:35,587
Missed. Try it again.

1611
01:02:35,620 --> 01:02:37,188
Ok.

1612
01:02:37,222 --> 01:02:39,357
Harder, now all together!

1613
01:02:40,692 --> 01:02:42,727
Come on, like
we rehearsed it.

1614
01:02:42,760 --> 01:02:44,528
Like Chorus Line.

1615
01:02:44,562 --> 01:02:47,498
Tug-of-war, next is
the three-legged-race.

1616
01:02:47,532 --> 01:02:48,598
Pull it up.

1617
01:02:48,632 --> 01:02:51,668
It's like, really
hard sir. You try it.

1618
01:02:51,702 --> 01:02:54,838
(grunting loudly)

1619
01:02:57,742 --> 01:02:58,742
Who's tough?

1620
01:02:58,775 --> 01:03:01,112
All he has to do is let go.

1621
01:03:01,145 --> 01:03:05,115
Like the Battle
of the Network Stars
with Telly Savalas.

1622
01:03:05,148 --> 01:03:06,883
Love ya, baby.

1623
01:03:07,585 --> 01:03:08,920
Grrr.

1624
01:03:12,657 --> 01:03:15,793
Ow, rope burns, ow.

1625
01:03:16,660 --> 01:03:18,562
That cuts it,
we're outta here!

1626
01:03:18,595 --> 01:03:21,398
Got anymore bread?

1627
01:03:23,533 --> 01:03:27,638
(robot makes baby coos)

1628
01:03:27,672 --> 01:03:28,672
They're cute at that age.

1629
01:03:28,705 --> 01:03:30,608
Oh, he's walking now.

1630
01:03:30,642 --> 01:03:33,845
Yeah, looks like
the amazing white guy.

1631
01:03:33,878 --> 01:03:35,145
I'd go in and I'll shave.

1632
01:03:35,178 --> 01:03:38,348
We're gonna try
the guns now, sir.

1633
01:03:38,382 --> 01:03:39,617
Hold your fire.

1634
01:03:39,650 --> 01:03:41,352
Head him away from the
planes with tear gas.

1635
01:03:41,385 --> 01:03:42,920
Anesthetic would do
better than tear gas.

1636
01:03:42,953 --> 01:03:44,355
Get some here on the double.

1637
01:03:44,388 --> 01:03:45,890
Captain, get reinforcements.

1638
01:03:45,923 --> 01:03:46,890
Well, we got his sister.

1639
01:03:46,923 --> 01:03:48,025
What are they
gonna do to him?

1640
01:03:48,058 --> 01:03:49,327
I don't know,
but I can't let him

1641
01:03:49,360 --> 01:03:50,528
loose among a million people.

1642
01:03:50,562 --> 01:03:51,595
Are you gonna
have to kill him?

1643
01:03:51,628 --> 01:03:52,830
If it comes down to it I will.

1644
01:03:52,863 --> 01:03:54,265
No, no. Please.

1645
01:03:54,298 --> 01:03:55,933
I'm responsible for
the lives of others first.

1646
01:03:55,967 --> 01:03:59,770
I'm responsible for the
lives of over 400 people.

1647
01:03:59,803 --> 01:04:00,938
See that Miss Manning
gets back to her hotel.

1648
01:04:00,972 --> 01:04:03,040
Yes sir. Come on sis.

1649
01:04:03,073 --> 01:04:05,342
I know how it is, come on.

1650
01:04:09,180 --> 01:04:10,113
What's that?

1651
01:04:10,147 --> 01:04:13,183
Is that some
kind of new plane?

1652
01:04:13,217 --> 01:04:15,485
Why are there so many
civilians milling around here?

1653
01:04:15,518 --> 01:04:17,488
(people screaming)

1654
01:04:17,522 --> 01:04:21,258
Where's the blue
Concourse, lady?

1655
01:04:21,292 --> 01:04:23,360
(gun shots)

1656
01:04:23,393 --> 01:04:25,028
Hey.

1657
01:04:26,263 --> 01:04:29,500
I'm workin' a chick
here, get your own.

1658
01:04:30,702 --> 01:04:33,103
We seem to be
aggravating him, sir.

1659
01:04:38,808 --> 01:04:41,412
Somebody get a slingshot.

1660
01:04:41,445 --> 01:04:44,082
(gun shots)

1661
01:04:49,220 --> 01:04:52,923
Up, up and away?
I don't think so.

1662
01:04:54,658 --> 01:04:56,460
Oh, he's eatin'
a la carte there.

1663
01:04:56,493 --> 01:04:58,795
(laughs)

1664
01:04:59,830 --> 01:05:02,333
Stay together cheeks,
stay together cheeks.

1665
01:05:02,367 --> 01:05:03,567
It's an emergency.

1666
01:05:03,600 --> 01:05:06,337
Hello, Ripley's?
Believe it or not.

1667
01:05:06,370 --> 01:05:07,438
This is Major Baird here.

1668
01:05:07,472 --> 01:05:09,740
Get all the aircraft
into the air at once.

1669
01:05:09,773 --> 01:05:12,910
That goes for the
Air Force Jets.

1670
01:05:12,943 --> 01:05:14,845
Everything, even the
stuff that doesn't fly.

1671
01:05:14,878 --> 01:05:17,782
Even people, everybody,
hop in the air.

1672
01:05:17,815 --> 01:05:20,017
OK, we'll meet at the
sports bar after this.

1673
01:05:20,050 --> 01:05:21,252
Right. Kill the giant.

1674
01:05:21,285 --> 01:05:26,290
(plane engine whirs)

1675
01:05:27,758 --> 01:05:31,462
Stock footage away.

1676
01:05:31,495 --> 01:05:34,698
More stock footage away.

1677
01:05:34,732 --> 01:05:38,468
(grunting)

1678
01:05:38,502 --> 01:05:40,603
I think he's hungry again.

1679
01:05:41,938 --> 01:05:44,475
Oh good, Mr.
Drysdale is here.

1680
01:05:47,478 --> 01:05:50,080
Stay clear in case he falls.

1681
01:05:51,548 --> 01:05:52,850
(gun shot)

1682
01:05:52,883 --> 01:05:55,687
Win yourself a Kewpie
doll. Two shots for a dollar.

1683
01:05:55,720 --> 01:05:57,555
Win somethin' for
the little lady.

1684
01:05:57,588 --> 01:05:58,888
Ding.

1685
01:05:58,922 --> 01:06:00,290
It's a Salad Shooter.

1686
01:06:00,323 --> 01:06:03,827
You get a choice of vegetable
or juice. Here it is.

1687
01:06:03,860 --> 01:06:05,195
It's got kind
of a smoky taste.

1688
01:06:05,228 --> 01:06:06,830
That's a strong
taste definitely.

1689
01:06:06,863 --> 01:06:08,532
Look out, men!

1690
01:06:10,300 --> 01:06:14,305
I think he just
saw the check, sir.

1691
01:06:14,338 --> 01:06:15,840
Come on, I dare you
to go sit in the ice.

1692
01:06:15,873 --> 01:06:18,108
You got a big 'ol head.

1693
01:06:18,142 --> 01:06:20,277
A big 'ol head, yeah.

1694
01:06:21,312 --> 01:06:23,747
Hi Gretchen,
hi Paul, hi Keeba.

1695
01:06:23,780 --> 01:06:25,315
Now, that's over.

1696
01:06:25,348 --> 01:06:27,452
City council is up
in arms about this.

1697
01:06:27,485 --> 01:06:30,153
Public has a right to
protection, you know.

1698
01:06:30,187 --> 01:06:32,188
We understand your
position Mr. Mayor.

1699
01:06:32,222 --> 01:06:33,590
I came off of Washington for the

1700
01:06:33,623 --> 01:06:35,993
sole purpose of
straightening this thing out.

1701
01:06:36,027 --> 01:06:38,795
We're here to decide
what's to be done.

1702
01:06:38,828 --> 01:06:40,563
He's safe now, is he?

1703
01:06:40,597 --> 01:06:42,600
Well, he's too weak to
break loose at the moment.

1704
01:06:42,633 --> 01:06:43,833
Well, let's go play on him.

1705
01:06:43,867 --> 01:06:45,668
He's out of touch with
his wild beast inside.

1706
01:06:45,703 --> 01:06:48,572
He lost considerable blood
in his attempt to escape.

1707
01:06:48,605 --> 01:06:51,142
Besides that, we've taken
extra precautions with him.

1708
01:06:51,175 --> 01:06:52,008
Extra special precautions.

1709
01:06:52,042 --> 01:06:53,977
Let me show you.

1710
01:06:54,010 --> 01:06:57,213
OK, so when can
I take him home Doc?

1711
01:06:57,247 --> 01:06:59,350
We keep a watch on
him night and day.

1712
01:06:59,383 --> 01:07:00,752
Those manacles were
especially wrought

1713
01:07:00,785 --> 01:07:02,787
to stand 10 times
this man of strength.

1714
01:07:02,820 --> 01:07:03,820
These are the
chains he forged in...

1715
01:07:03,853 --> 01:07:05,088
We have them
anchored in cement

1716
01:07:05,122 --> 01:07:06,357
weighing two tons and
some 12 feet in the air.

1717
01:07:06,390 --> 01:07:07,658
(sniffing)

1718
01:07:07,692 --> 01:07:08,858
Guards have been doubled

1719
01:07:08,892 --> 01:07:10,728
and we keep a reserve
force in standby duty.

1720
01:07:10,762 --> 01:07:12,095
Smells like a
monkey house in here.

1721
01:07:12,128 --> 01:07:13,230
There's not much
chance he'll give us

1722
01:07:13,263 --> 01:07:15,065
anymore trouble
for the time being.

1723
01:07:15,098 --> 01:07:17,133
Well you certainly
have taken measures.

1724
01:07:17,167 --> 01:07:19,570
But you can't expect to
keep him here for life.

1725
01:07:19,603 --> 01:07:23,007
Have you any idea what'll
happen to him eventually?

1726
01:07:23,040 --> 01:07:24,608
Well, that depends.

1727
01:07:24,642 --> 01:07:25,777
On you, the viewer.

1728
01:07:25,810 --> 01:07:28,112
Do you see any hope
that he'll ever improve?

1729
01:07:28,145 --> 01:07:30,982
Well, I'd rather have
you ask Dr. Carmichael.

1730
01:07:31,015 --> 01:07:32,783
Dr. Carmichael.

1731
01:07:32,817 --> 01:07:35,185
Yes, this is Carmichael.

1732
01:07:35,218 --> 01:07:37,722
Dr. Carmichael, Mayor.

1733
01:07:37,755 --> 01:07:38,788
Can I call you Hoagie?

1734
01:07:38,822 --> 01:07:39,957
General Mills.

1735
01:07:39,990 --> 01:07:41,392
Major Nelson.

1736
01:07:41,425 --> 01:07:42,893
What can you tell 'em
about your patient, doctor?

1737
01:07:42,927 --> 01:07:44,262
Well he's tall for his age.

1738
01:07:44,295 --> 01:07:48,132
Well, the big
question now is his mind.

1739
01:07:48,165 --> 01:07:50,267
He may be suffering
from amnesia,

1740
01:07:50,300 --> 01:07:53,637
shell-shock, loss of memory,
whatever you want to call it.

1741
01:07:53,670 --> 01:07:54,805
In that case, we have techniques

1742
01:07:54,838 --> 01:07:56,507
now that'll bring him out of it.

1743
01:07:56,540 --> 01:08:01,278
On the other hand, if his brain
tissue has suffered injury,

1744
01:08:01,312 --> 01:08:04,482
he'll be a psychopathic case
and a menace until he dies.

1745
01:08:04,515 --> 01:08:05,850
Like Jerry Lewis?

1746
01:08:05,883 --> 01:08:08,152
Is there any way of telling?

1747
01:08:08,185 --> 01:08:09,687
We're starting him
on group on Monday.

1748
01:08:09,720 --> 01:08:10,720
An examination can do that.

1749
01:08:10,753 --> 01:08:12,490
How soon can you
proceed with it?

1750
01:08:12,523 --> 01:08:13,923
Almost immediately.
(phone rings)

1751
01:08:13,957 --> 01:08:14,925
After I take this call.

1752
01:08:14,958 --> 01:08:16,360
Please do so.

1753
01:08:16,393 --> 01:08:17,495
We'll decide what's to
be done with Manning

1754
01:08:17,528 --> 01:08:20,832
as soon as we have
the Doctor's findings.

1755
01:08:20,865 --> 01:08:21,832
We'll keep you posted, John.

1756
01:08:21,865 --> 01:08:23,000
(yawns loudly)

1757
01:08:23,033 --> 01:08:23,933
And I'll check
with you before

1758
01:08:23,967 --> 01:08:24,968
I return to Washington, Major.

1759
01:08:25,002 --> 01:08:26,137
Yes, sir.

1760
01:08:26,170 --> 01:08:28,438
Just end the scene
for cripes sake.

1761
01:08:28,472 --> 01:08:29,640
Drag it out.

1762
01:08:29,673 --> 01:08:31,942
Did I tell you that a man's
life depends on this plasma?

1763
01:08:31,975 --> 01:08:32,877
A big man.

1764
01:08:32,910 --> 01:08:34,378
I know 10 gallons is a lot,

1765
01:08:34,412 --> 01:08:36,747
but Dr. Carmichael explained
the case to you this morning.

1766
01:08:36,780 --> 01:08:38,048
And that's just for his hat.

1767
01:08:38,082 --> 01:08:39,115
I can't sit here
and argue about it,

1768
01:08:39,150 --> 01:08:42,185
it's needed right away.

1769
01:08:42,218 --> 01:08:43,487
Just a moment please.

1770
01:08:43,520 --> 01:08:45,055
Would you tell
him how big he is?

1771
01:08:45,088 --> 01:08:46,223
Dr. Carmichael.

1772
01:08:46,257 --> 01:08:48,058
Remember he's
big, keep lookin' up.

1773
01:08:50,160 --> 01:08:52,062
Will you talk to the
Red Cross people please?

1774
01:08:52,095 --> 01:08:54,165
They refuse to send
anymore plasma for Glenn.

1775
01:08:54,198 --> 01:08:55,900
They say we've used
too much already.

1776
01:08:55,933 --> 01:08:58,202
No, no. Tell
them I'm not here.

1777
01:08:58,235 --> 01:09:00,770
Tell them I paid the bill.

1778
01:09:00,803 --> 01:09:03,640
Help me out over here.

1779
01:09:03,673 --> 01:09:07,110
Seems like everyone is
afraid of the Red Cross.

1780
01:09:08,678 --> 01:09:10,882
Major Baird.

1781
01:09:10,915 --> 01:09:12,115
Annette and I will
see that you get

1782
01:09:12,148 --> 01:09:14,285
back all the blood we
used for this case.

1783
01:09:14,318 --> 01:09:16,387
Now, you'll send over
what we need immediately.

1784
01:09:16,420 --> 01:09:19,657
Type. Type O. Type
oh my God, he's huge.

1785
01:09:19,690 --> 01:09:21,325
Goodbye.

1786
01:09:22,258 --> 01:09:23,693
They'll have it
here in half an hour.

1787
01:09:23,727 --> 01:09:24,495
Or it's free.

1788
01:09:24,528 --> 01:09:28,665
Major, wait. I
want to thank you.

1789
01:09:28,698 --> 01:09:29,867
Forget it.

1790
01:09:29,900 --> 01:09:31,302
I'm sorry for what
I said yesterday.

1791
01:09:31,335 --> 01:09:33,770
I know you've tried to save
Glenn as much as you could.

1792
01:09:33,803 --> 01:09:35,338
You look tired, Joyce.

1793
01:09:35,372 --> 01:09:36,073
Sleep.

1794
01:09:36,107 --> 01:09:37,440
Isn't it time you went home?

1795
01:09:37,473 --> 01:09:39,277
I suppose it is.

1796
01:09:39,310 --> 01:09:40,510
There's an army
car waiting outside

1797
01:09:40,543 --> 01:09:42,613
if you would like
to go home in that.

1798
01:09:42,647 --> 01:09:45,682
With a soldier to
see me to the door?

1799
01:09:45,715 --> 01:09:47,117
This time, I'll do it myself.

1800
01:09:47,150 --> 01:09:50,955
That was an easy date.
General Smooth makes his move.

1801
01:09:50,988 --> 01:09:52,957
(snoring loudly)

1802
01:09:52,990 --> 01:09:55,325
Get your paws off my sister.

1803
01:09:55,358 --> 01:09:58,095
(suspenseful classical music)

1804
01:10:01,965 --> 01:10:04,368
Sure is big huh?
My little brother.

1805
01:10:04,402 --> 01:10:07,572
Can you believe it? How ironic.

1806
01:10:07,605 --> 01:10:11,408
"Had to leave, in the
circus end again. Ooh."

1807
01:10:16,947 --> 01:10:19,683
I just dreamed I was little.

1808
01:10:23,453 --> 01:10:26,590
Chains. My baby's got
me locked up in chains.

1809
01:10:26,623 --> 01:10:29,827
And they are the kind
that you can see.

1810
01:10:31,162 --> 01:10:33,563
Help, there's a normal
size person under my bed.

1811
01:10:33,597 --> 01:10:36,067
This is an
electro-encephalograph.

1812
01:10:36,100 --> 01:10:37,033
So don't touch it.

1813
01:10:37,067 --> 01:10:38,002
It records impulses set off

1814
01:10:38,035 --> 01:10:39,403
by different parts of the brain.

1815
01:10:39,437 --> 01:10:40,670
And it makes great taffy.

1816
01:10:40,703 --> 01:10:43,007
Records him on this paper,
making these wavy lines.

1817
01:10:43,040 --> 01:10:44,140
You hit this key,
you get a Samba.

1818
01:10:44,173 --> 01:10:45,375
We're gonna try to stimulate

1819
01:10:45,408 --> 01:10:48,312
your brother's mind
with various ideas.

1820
01:10:48,345 --> 01:10:50,647
If one happens to arouse
a response, it will cause

1821
01:10:50,680 --> 01:10:53,583
a tiny electric current
to occur in his brain

1822
01:10:53,617 --> 01:10:56,720
and that in turn will be greatly
amplified by this machine.

1823
01:10:56,753 --> 01:10:57,520
And greatly appreciated.

1824
01:10:57,553 --> 01:10:59,857
That will register
on this paper.

1825
01:10:59,890 --> 01:11:01,225
And what will that tell us?

1826
01:11:01,258 --> 01:11:02,558
Nothin', it's just for kicks.

1827
01:11:02,592 --> 01:11:06,797
If he responds to anything,
it means curable amnesia.

1828
01:11:06,830 --> 01:11:07,698
Or something, I forget.

1829
01:11:07,732 --> 01:11:09,767
If not...

1830
01:11:09,800 --> 01:11:10,567
I understand.

1831
01:11:10,600 --> 01:11:12,302
Good, will you
explain it to me?

1832
01:11:12,335 --> 01:11:13,270
I can't make heads
or tails out of it.

1833
01:11:13,303 --> 01:11:15,538
Will you get this out please?

1834
01:11:17,742 --> 01:11:20,943
Oh no. It's being repossessed
by the Military again.

1835
01:11:20,978 --> 01:11:22,112
(laughs)

1836
01:11:22,145 --> 01:11:23,647
Little Army joke.

1837
01:11:23,680 --> 01:11:27,383
Ah, to be young and be able
to push around a big machine.

1838
01:11:27,417 --> 01:11:31,588
OK. Surprise. Happy
Birthday to you...

1839
01:11:31,622 --> 01:11:35,525
Dr. Richardson, how's
the examination coming?

1840
01:11:35,558 --> 01:11:37,127
My nurse fell down (mumbles).

1841
01:11:37,160 --> 01:11:39,095
Getting along well,
I'll be down in a minute.

1842
01:11:39,128 --> 01:11:41,398
I'll get your
instrument ready.

1843
01:11:41,432 --> 01:11:43,000
Will you hook this up please?

1844
01:11:43,033 --> 01:11:45,102
Bill, bring the projector.

1845
01:11:45,135 --> 01:11:48,438
Get that cat
out of here. Meow.

1846
01:11:48,472 --> 01:11:50,573
Set it up over there.

1847
01:11:50,607 --> 01:11:52,342
Hey, check out
this blackhead,

1848
01:11:52,375 --> 01:11:55,145
